// Generated by scripts/build-manual.py from the course manual PDF. Do not edit by hand.
// Server-only: section text is sent to students who own the stage.
export const manualStage1: Record<string, string> = {
"welcome": "<p>Somewhere near you, there is a bar. A park pull-up bar, a scaffolding pipe, a school playground frame. It has been there for years. Thousands of people have walked past it. A handful have jumped up, hung for a few seconds, and dropped off again.</p><p>This course is about becoming the person who can do something extraordinary on that bar — and then the person who can teach anyone else to do the same.</p><p>Here is the thing nobody tells beginners: the gap between &quot;can&#x27;t do a single pull-up&quot; and &quot;can do fifteen&quot; is not talent. It is not genetics. It is not even mostly effort. It is <strong>sequence</strong>. The right movements, in the right order, at the right difficulty, repeated long enough for the body to adapt. Get the sequence right and progress feels almost inevitable. Get it wrong and you can train hard for two years and end up injured, frustrated, and convinced you are one of those people who &quot;just can&#x27;t.&quot;</p><p>Stage 1 is the sequence.</p><h4><strong>Who this stage is for</strong></h4><p><strong>If you are training for yourself:</strong> Stage 1 is complete on its own. Finish it and you will own a handful of movements properly, understand what your body is doing and why, and be able to write yourself a training week that keeps moving forward instead of running in circles.</p><p><strong>If you are working toward trainer certification:</strong> Stage 1 is the floor, not a formality. You cannot coach a movement you cannot perform. You cannot correct a fault you cannot see. Everything in Stage 2 and every client conversation in Stage 3 assumes the material in these pages is <em>in your body</em>, not just in your notes.</p><h4><strong>What you will be able to do by the end</strong></h4><ol><li>Explain what calisthenics is, how its movements are classified, and why leverage is the whole game.</li><li>Explain how the human body actually gets stronger — and use that to make training decisions instead of guessing.</li><li>Name the major pushing and pulling muscles and identify, on sight, which ones a movement is loading.</li><li>Screen a body for the restrictions that make foundational movements unsafe.</li><li>Perform the six foundational patterns with controlled, honest form.</li><li>Control your shoulder blades deliberately — and spot instantly when someone else cannot.</li><li>Breathe and brace under load instead of holding your breath and hoping.</li><li>Write, run, and progress a balanced beginner training week for yourself or someone else.</li></ol><h4><strong>How to use this manual</strong></h4><p>Do not read it like a novel. Read one module, then go train that module for two to three weeks before moving on. The reading takes a few hours. The adaptation takes <strong>8 to 12 weeks minimum</strong>, and there is no version of this where you skip that part.</p><p>Throughout the manual you will find four recurring boxes:</p><aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p><strong>COACH&#x27;S EYE</strong> — what an experienced trainer notices that a beginner misses.</p><p><strong>MYTH VS REALITY</strong> — a widely repeated belief, and what is actually true.</p><p><strong>TRY THIS NOW</strong> — a 60-second experiment. Put the book down and do it. These are the fastest way to turn reading into understanding.</p><p><strong>FROM THE FLOOR</strong> — a short real-world scenario, and what to do about it.</p></aside><p>Let&#x27;s begin.</p>",
"1.1.1": "<p>The word comes from two Greek roots: <em>kalos</em>, meaning beautiful, and <em>sthenos</em>, meaning strength. <strong>Beautiful strength.</strong> That naming is not decoration — it tells you what the discipline has always valued. Not the biggest number lifted, but the most control demonstrated.</p><p>Ancient Greek and Indian training traditions both built their systems on bodyweight movement. Indian wrestlers were performing thousands of <em>dands</em> and <em>baithaks</em> — pushup and squat variations — centuries before anyone designed a weight machine. Military physical training worldwide has always run on pushups, pull-ups, and running, for the simple reason that it works and it costs nothing.</p><p>What changed recently is the sport. In the early 2000s, videos started coming out of parks in Eastern Europe, Latin America, and New York City of people doing things on bars that looked physically impossible — spinning, levitating sideways, holding their bodies horizontal in mid-air. The internet did the rest. Within fifteen years there were world championships, national teams, and judged competitive formats.</p><p>And now there is you, holding a certification manual for it.</p>",
"1.1.2": "<p>Calisthenics is strength training where the load is your own body, and difficulty is changed by <strong>altering leverage</strong> <strong>rather than adding weight</strong>.</p><p>Read that second half again. It is the entire discipline compressed into one clause.</p><p>In a weight room, progression is arithmetic. 40 kilos becomes 45 becomes 50. In calisthenics, progression is geometry. You change the angle of your body, the length of the lever, the number of limbs sharing the load, the speed of movement, the stability of the surface.</p><aside class=\"callout callout-try-this-now\"><p class=\"callout-label\">TRY THIS NOW</p><p>Stand up. Put your hands on a wall at chest height and do a pushup. Easy, almost weightless. Now put your hands on a chair or table. Harder. Now on the floor. Harder again. Now put your feet up on the chair, hands on the floor. Harder still. You did not touch a single weight. You changed the percentage of your bodyweight travelling through your hands — from roughly 20% at the wall to over 70% with your feet elevated. <strong>That is leverage. That is the whole sport.</strong></p></aside><p>Once you understand this, a strange and wonderful thing becomes true: <strong>there is no such thing as an exercise that is</strong> <strong>too hard.</strong> There is only an exercise you have not yet found the right regression for. A 65-year-old beginner and a national champion can train the identical movement pattern in the same session, at completely different leverages, and both be appropriately challenged.</p><p>This is also why calisthenics coaching is a genuine skill. Anyone can tell a client to add 5 kilos. Knowing which of thirty possible leverage adjustments this particular body needs today is what you are here to learn.</p>",
"1.1.3": "<p>Be honest about both. A trainer who oversells their method loses credibility the first time a client asks a hard question.</p><p><strong>What calisthenics builds exceptionally well:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Relative strength</strong> — strength in proportion to bodyweight. This is a genuinely different quality from absolute strength. A 60 kg athlete doing a one-arm pull-up is displaying something a 120 kg powerlifter almost certainly cannot, even though the powerlifter moves far more total weight.</li><li><strong>Body control and coordination</strong> — the nervous system learning to organise many joints simultaneously under load. This transfers to almost every sport.</li><li><strong>Joint integrity</strong> — most calisthenics movements are closed-chain and multi-joint, meaning the small stabilising muscles around the shoulder, elbow, wrist, and hip get trained alongside the big prime movers. Machines isolate; bars integrate.</li><li><strong>Strength through range</strong> — mobility that holds up under load, not just passive flexibility.</li><li><strong>Muscular endurance</strong> — high-repetition bodyweight work builds a work capacity that heavy low-rep training simply does not.</li><li><strong>Independence</strong> — a trainee who needs a bar and a floor can train on holiday, during a move, after a job loss, in a village with no gym. Consistency is the single biggest predictor of long-term results, and calisthenics removes most of the excuses.</li></ul><p><strong>What it does less readily, and you should say so out loud:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Maximum absolute strength.</strong> If someone&#x27;s goal is a 200 kg deadlift, bodyweight training is not the fastest route.</li><li><strong>Heavy direct loading of the posterior chain.</strong> Hamstrings, glutes, and spinal erectors are genuinely hard to load heavily with bodyweight alone. This is the most common gap in a calisthenics athlete&#x27;s body, and this course addresses it directly with hinge patterns in Module 1.6 and street lifting in Stage 2.</li><li><strong>Precise progressive overload.</strong> Adding 2.5 kg is a small, measurable step. Moving from knee pushups to full pushups is a large, unmeasurable one. Good programming has to bridge those gaps deliberately — Module 1.11 shows you how.</li></ul>",
"1.1.4": "<aside class=\"callout callout-myth-vs-reality\"><p class=\"callout-label\">MYTH VS REALITY</p><p><strong>&quot;Calisthenics can&#x27;t build real muscle.&quot;</strong> Reality: mechanical tension, metabolic stress, and progressive overload build muscle. Bodyweight training delivers all three. Look at any high-level ring gymnast — most have never touched a bench press, and most are more muscular through the upper body than the average gym regular. The limitation is not the method; it is programming that never progresses.</p><p><strong>&quot;You need to train every day to get good.&quot;</strong> Reality: you need to <em>recover</em> to get good. Training is the stimulus; adaptation happens during rest. Three well-designed sessions per week beats seven scattered ones, almost every time, for a beginner.</p><p><strong>&quot;Stretching before training prevents injury.&quot;</strong> Reality: long static stretches on cold muscle temporarily reduce force output and have never shown a clear injury-prevention benefit. A proper warm-up raises tissue temperature and rehearses the movement. Save the long holds for after. Module 1.5 covers this fully.</p><p><strong>&quot;Calisthenics is safer than weights, so beginners can&#x27;t get hurt.&quot;</strong> Reality: bodyweight training puts complete beginners into overhead, hanging, and inverted positions their shoulders have never experienced, often with no load-management plan at all. It is not inherently safer. It is safer <em>when coached properly</em> — which is the point of this certification.</p><p><strong>&quot;If you can&#x27;t do a pull-up, just do pull-ups until you can.&quot;</strong> Reality: this is how people spend eight months making no progress. You cannot practise a movement you cannot perform. You build the prerequisites, then the movement appears. Module 1.6 gives you the ladder.</p></aside>",
"1.1.5": "<p>Trainees and trainers need two ways of classifying movement, and they serve different purposes.</p><p><strong>MAP A — By body region.</strong> Useful for a quick check that nothing has been forgotten.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Region</strong></th><th><strong>Examples</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Upper body</td><td>Pushups, pull-ups, dips, rows</td></tr><tr><td>Lower body</td><td>Squats, lunges, split squats, calf raises</td></tr><tr><td>Core</td><td>Planks, hollow holds, hanging leg raises, L-sits</td></tr><tr><td>Full body</td><td>Burpees, bear crawls, mountain climbers</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><strong>MAP B — By movement pattern.</strong> This is the map that actually builds programs, and the one this course uses from here on.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Pattern</strong></th><th><strong>Direction relative to torso</strong></th><th><strong>Foundational example</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Horizontal push</td><td>Away from chest, perpendicular</td><td>Pushup</td></tr><tr><td>Horizontal pull</td><td>Toward chest, perpendicular</td><td>Inverted row</td></tr><tr><td>Vertical push</td><td>Overhead, in line with torso</td><td>Dip, pike pushup</td></tr><tr><td>Vertical pull</td><td>Down from overhead, in line</td><td>Pull-up, chin-up</td></tr><tr><td>Squat</td><td>Knee-dominant flexion/extension</td><td>Squat, split squat</td></tr><tr><td>Hinge</td><td>Hip-dominant flexion/extension</td><td>Hip bridge, Nordic curl</td></tr><tr><td>Core / anti-movement</td><td>Resisting motion, not creating it</td><td>Plank, hollow hold</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><strong>Why the second map wins.</strong> Build a program by body region and you will train &quot;chest&quot; and &quot;back&quot; and never notice you have programmed four horizontal pushes and zero vertical pulls. Patterns make the gap visible immediately.</p><p>Out of this falls a rule you will use for the rest of your career:</p><p class=\"rule\"><strong>Every push pattern gets a matching pull pattern.</strong></p><p>Ignore it and here is the timeline: months 1–6, your client feels great and gets stronger. Months 6–12, their shoulders start rounding forward and their posture changes. Months 12–18, a nagging pinch appears at the front of the shoulder during overhead work. Month 20, they stop training because it hurts.</p><p>The most common injury in this sport is not dramatic. It is slow, it is preventable, and it is caused by programming that had too much pushing in it.</p><aside class=\"callout callout-coach-s-eye\"><p class=\"callout-label\">COACH&#x27;S EYE</p><p>When you get a new client&#x27;s current routine, do not read it for what is in it. Read it for what is missing. Count patterns, not exercises. Nine times out of ten the missing pattern is a horizontal pull, and nine times out of ten that is where their problem is coming from.</p></aside>",
"1.1.6": "<p>Calisthenics as a competitive sport splits into four disciplines. Stage 1 does not train them separately — a beginner needs general strength first — but you need the map now, because it determines where each trainee goes in Stage 2.</p><p><strong>ENDURANCE.</strong> Maximum reps or maximum hold time. Pull-up sets against the clock, AMRAP formats, high-rep circuits. Tests work capacity and pain tolerance. The most accessible lane for beginners and the most common competitive format in community events.</p><p><strong>STREET LIFTING.</strong> Bodyweight movements loaded with external weight — weighted pull-ups, weighted dips, weighted squats, weighted muscle-ups. Tests maximum strength. The lane that fixes calisthenics&#x27; absolute-strength weakness.</p><p><strong>STATICS.</strong> Isometric holds fighting gravity&#x27;s leverage — planche, front lever, back lever, human flag. Tests strength at extreme leverage plus connective-tissue resilience. The slowest lane to develop and the most spectacular to watch.</p><p><strong>FREESTYLE.</strong> Creative, dynamic, flowing movement on and around the bar. Judged on difficulty, execution, originality, and flow. Tests everything at once, plus courage.</p><aside class=\"callout callout-coach-s-eye\"><p class=\"callout-label\">COACH&#x27;S EYE</p><p>The most common mistake a new trainee makes is picking a lane in week one. A seventeen-year-old sees a planche video and wants to train planche exclusively. A trainer who allows this is failing them: the planche needs a base of general pushing strength, scapular control, and wrist conditioning that takes a year to build. Give them the base, and the skill arrives <em>faster</em> than it would have with a year of impatient specialisation. Say this to them early and say it clearly — it is one of the most valuable things you will ever tell a client.</p></aside>",
"1.1.7": "<p>You need remarkably little. Here is the honest hierarchy.</p><p><strong>Essential</strong></p><ul><li>A floor. Preferably not concrete for the sake of your wrists and knees.</li><li>A pull-up bar. A park bar, a doorway bar, scaffolding. Must hold your weight without question — check it every single time.</li></ul><p><strong>High value, low cost</strong></p><ul><li>Parallel bars or dip bars. Unlocks dips, L-sits, support holds. Park installations, or two sturdy chairs at a pinch.</li><li>Resistance bands. The single best assistance tool for pull-ups and dips. Two or three strengths cover everything in Stage 1.</li><li>Parallettes. Low, shoulder-width bars. Kinder on the wrists than the floor, unlocks L-sits and pushup variations. Easily built from PVC pipe.</li></ul><p><strong>Nice to have</strong></p><ul><li>Gymnastic rings. Enormous versatility, but genuinely demanding — not a Stage 1 tool for most beginners.</li><li>Weight belt or vest, for Stage 2 street lifting.</li><li>A gym mat, for floor and inverted work.</li><li>Chalk, for grip in humid conditions.</li></ul><aside class=\"callout callout-from-the-floor\"><p class=\"callout-label\">FROM THE FLOOR</p><p>A client tells you they can&#x27;t start training because they don&#x27;t have a gym membership yet, and asks what equipment to buy first. Correct answer: nothing. Start today, on the floor, with the bar in the park down the road. Buy a resistance band in week three if pull- up assistance is the bottleneck. Anyone who tells a beginner they need equipment before they can begin is selling something.</p></aside>",
"1.1-quiz": "<p>Five questions, ordered from straightforward to challenging.</p><ol><li>In calisthenics, difficulty is primarily increased by:</li></ol><ul class=\"options\"><li>A) Adding external weight in small increments</li><li>B) Altering leverage, range, speed, stability, and the number of limbs carrying the load</li><li>C) Increasing the number of training days per week</li><li>D) Shortening rest periods between sets</li></ul><ol start=\"2\"><li>Which lane tests maximal strength?</li></ol><ul class=\"options\"><li>A) Endurance</li><li>B) Statics</li><li>C) Street lifting</li><li>D) Freestyle</li></ul><ol start=\"3\"><li>Which of the following is the strongest argument for classifying exercises by movement pattern rather than body region?</li></ol><ul class=\"options\"><li>A) Patterns are easier for beginners to remember</li><li>B) Body-region classification cannot describe core exercises</li><li>C) Patterns make programming gaps, such as missing vertical pulls, immediately visible</li><li>D) Movement patterns correspond directly to competition formats</li></ul><ol start=\"4\"><li>A client&#x27;s program contains pushups, dips, pike pushups, squats, and planks. The most significant problem is:</li></ol><ul class=\"options\"><li>A) Insufficient core volume</li><li>B) No pulling patterns at all, risking shoulder problems over months</li><li>C) Too much lower-body work</li><li>D) No full-body exercises</li></ul><ol start=\"5\"><li>A trainee with 14 months of experience wants to train only the planche. The best coaching response is to:</li></ol><ul class=\"options\"><li>A) Refuse and insist on another year of general training</li><li>B) Agree without conditions, since motivation matters most</li><li>C) Make it the priority while keeping general strength at maintenance and building tissue tolerance first</li><li>D) Substitute handstand work and say nothing about the planche</li></ul>",
"1.2.1": "<p>Most people train by copying. They copy a routine from a video, follow it until it stops working, then copy a different one. It can work for a while. It cannot work forever, and it cannot be taught to anyone else.</p><p>Trainers work differently. A trainer understands the underlying principles and <em>derives</em> the program from them. That means when a client&#x27;s situation is unusual — injured wrist, night shifts, no bar, eight weeks until a competition — the trainer can build something appropriate from first principles instead of hunting for a template.</p><p>This module is short. It is also the most leveraged material in Stage 1.</p><p>Training does not make you stronger. <strong>Recovering from training makes you stronger.</strong></p><p>Here is the actual sequence:</p><ol><li><strong>Stimulus.</strong> You train. Muscle fibres experience tension, small amounts of damage occur, and the nervous system is challenged to coordinate something difficult.</li><li><strong>Fatigue.</strong> Performance temporarily <em>drops</em>. You are weaker immediately after a hard session, not stronger.</li><li><strong>Recovery.</strong> Over the following 24 to 72 hours, tissue repairs and the nervous system consolidates what it learned.</li><li><strong>Adaptation.</strong> The body rebuilds slightly beyond its previous capacity, so that the same stimulus will be easier next time. This is called supercompensation.</li><li><strong>Repeat — at the right moment.</strong> Train again near the peak of that new capacity and you build upward. Train too soon, repeatedly, and you accumulate fatigue faster than you adapt. Train too late and the adaptation fades.</li></ol><p>Two failure modes fall out of this:</p><p><strong>Too much, too often.</strong> Fatigue outpaces recovery. Performance stalls, then declines. Sleep gets worse, motivation drops, small aches appear and linger. Beginners rarely reach true overtraining, but they very commonly reach under- recovery.</p><p><strong>Too little, too rarely.</strong> No stimulus large enough to require adaptation. The trainee does the same three sets of ten for a year and looks the same in month twelve as month one.</p><aside class=\"callout callout-coach-s-eye\"><p class=\"callout-label\">COACH&#x27;S EYE</p><p>When a client is not progressing, everyone&#x27;s instinct — theirs and yours — is to add more training. Roughly half the time, the correct intervention is <em>less</em> training and more sleep. Ask about sleep, work stress, and food before you ever add a fourth session. You will look like a genius surprisingly often.</p></aside>",
"1.2.2": "<p>If a training program has one law, it is this: <strong>to keep adapting, the demand must keep increasing.</strong> The body adapts to what is required of it and not one bit more.</p><p>In calisthenics you have six levers to increase demand. Know all six, because when one is unavailable, another will be.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Lever</strong></th><th><strong>How it works</strong></th><th><strong>Best used for</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>1. More reps</strong></td><td>3×8 becomes 3×9 becomes 3×10</td><td>The primary beginner method. Simple and trackable.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>2. More sets</strong></td><td>3×10 becomes 4×10</td><td>Adding volume once technique is solid</td></tr><tr><td><strong>3. Harder leverage</strong></td><td>Incline pushup becomes full pushup</td><td>The calisthenics-specific method. Most important long term.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>4. Slower tempo</strong></td><td>1 second down becomes 4 seconds down</td><td>Adding difficulty without changing the exercise</td></tr><tr><td><strong>5. Less rest</strong></td><td>90 seconds becomes 60 seconds</td><td>Endurance and work capacity, not strength</td></tr><tr><td><strong>6. Greater range</strong></td><td>Half squat becomes full-depth squat</td><td>Almost always the first lever to pull, and the most ignored</td></tr></tbody></table></div><aside class=\"callout callout-coach-s-eye\"><p class=\"callout-label\">COACH&#x27;S EYE</p><p>Lever 6 is free progress that almost everyone leaves on the table. Before you make an exercise harder, make it <em>complete</em>. A client doing half-range pushups who learns to touch their chest to the floor has just increased their training stimulus significantly without changing a single variable on paper. Always buy full range before you buy extra difficulty.</p></aside>",
"1.2.3": "<p>The body adapts specifically to the demand placed on it. Train high reps, get endurance. Train heavy and slow, get maximal strength. Train a skill, get better at that skill — and only modestly better at similar ones.</p><p>This sounds obvious and is constantly violated. A trainee who wants a muscle-up but trains only high-rep pushups is not training for their goal. A client who wants to look more muscular but only does 30-second circuits is training the wrong quality.</p><p>Specificity has a second, more useful edge, though: <strong>early on, almost everything transfers.</strong> A total beginner improves at everything from any reasonable training. This is why Stage 1 is general. Specificity matters enormously at advanced levels and barely at all in month one — so use the beginner window to build broad competence before it closes.</p>",
"1.2.4": "<p>Ask a new trainee why they went from three pull-ups to seven in two months and they will say &quot;my back got stronger.&quot; They are mostly wrong, and understanding why makes you a better coach.</p><p>Early strength gains are predominantly <strong>neurological</strong>, not muscular. The muscle has not grown much in eight weeks. What has changed:</p><ul><li><strong>Motor unit recruitment.</strong> The nervous system learns to activate more muscle fibres simultaneously. Untrained people cannot access a large fraction of their own muscle.</li><li><strong>Rate coding.</strong> Signals fire faster, producing more force from the same tissue.</li><li><strong>Coordination and reduced co-contraction.</strong> Beginners tense antagonist muscles that fight the movement. Practice removes that self-sabotage.</li><li><strong>Skill.</strong> Knowing the groove, the bracing, the breath, the timing.</li></ul><p>Two practical consequences:</p><p><strong>First: frequency beats volume for beginners.</strong> If early gains are skill acquisition, then the goal is <em>practice repetitions</em>, distributed often. Three full-body sessions a week — each pattern trained three times — beats one brutal session that leaves them wrecked for four days. This is exactly why Stage 1 programs are full-body, three days a week.</p><p><strong>Second: technique quality is not a nicety, it is the stimulus.</strong> Every sloppy rep is a repetition of a bad motor pattern. The nervous system does not distinguish between practising well and practising badly; it just gets better at whatever you actually did. This is why &quot;stop the set when form breaks&quot; is not a safety platitude — it is a training- efficiency instruction.</p><aside class=\"callout callout-try-this-now\"><p class=\"callout-label\">TRY THIS NOW</p><p>Do five pushups as fast as you can. Note how they feel. Rest a minute. Now do five pushups taking three seconds to lower, pausing one second at the bottom, and two seconds to push up. Same five reps. The second set was dramatically harder and you changed nothing except time. That is tempo — lever 4 — and most beginners never touch it.</p></aside>",
"1.2.5": "<p>How hard should a set be? &quot;As hard as possible&quot; is the intuitive answer and it is wrong.</p><p>Use <strong>reps in reserve (RIR)</strong> — how many more reps you could have done with good form when you stopped.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>RIR</strong></th><th><strong>Meaning</strong></th><th><strong>When to use</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0</td><td>Absolute failure; could not do another</td><td>Rarely. Occasional testing only.</td></tr><tr><td>1–2</td><td>Very hard; 1–2 left in the tank</td><td>Last set of a main movement</td></tr><tr><td>2–3</td><td>Hard but controlled</td><td><strong>Stage 1 default for most sets</strong></td></tr><tr><td>4+</td><td>Comfortable</td><td>Warm-ups, technique practice, deload weeks</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Why not train to failure constantly? Three reasons. The final reps before failure are technically the worst, so you are practising bad form. Failure generates disproportionate fatigue relative to its extra stimulus, costing you quality in later exercises. And it is psychologically unsustainable — clients who leave every session destroyed do not come back for year two.</p><p><strong>Stage 1 rule: leave 2–3 reps in reserve on most sets, 1–2 on your last set of a key movement, and stop</strong> <strong>immediately the moment form degrades regardless of what the number says.</strong></p>",
"1.2.6": "<p>You will see tempo written as four numbers, e.g. <strong>3-1-1-0</strong>. Read them in order of what the body is doing:</p><ol><li><strong>Eccentric</strong> — the lowering phase (3 seconds)</li><li><strong>Bottom pause</strong> — at the stretched position (1 second)</li><li><strong>Concentric</strong> — the lifting phase (1 second)</li><li><strong>Top pause</strong> — at the finish (0 seconds)</li></ol><p>So a pushup at 3-1-1-0 means: lower for three seconds, pause one second with the chest near the floor, push up in one second, no pause at the top, immediately begin the next rep.</p><p>Three tempos worth knowing:</p><ul><li><strong>2-0-1-0</strong> — general default. Controlled, no wasted time.</li><li><strong>3-1-1-0</strong> — technique and control emphasis. Excellent for beginners learning a pattern.</li><li><strong>5-0-1-0</strong> — heavy eccentric focus. The core of negative-rep training for building toward a first pull-up.</li></ul><p>The eccentric phase is where most strength adaptation and most muscle-building tension live. It is also the phase every impatient trainee rushes. If you take one programming habit from this module, make it this: <strong>slow the lowering phase</strong> <strong>down.</strong></p>",
"1.2.7": "<p>Two clients, same age, same starting strength, same program. After twelve weeks one has doubled their pull-ups and the other has added one. Neither did anything wrong.</p><p>Variation comes from:</p><ul><li><strong>Genetics</strong> — muscle fibre distribution, tendon insertion points, limb proportions. A trainee with long arms has a mechanically longer pull-up; that is not a character flaw.</li><li><strong>Training history</strong> — a former swimmer&#x27;s shoulders are not a desk worker&#x27;s shoulders.</li><li><strong>Recovery capacity</strong> — sleep, stress, nutrition, age, health status.</li><li><strong>Body composition</strong> — in bodyweight training, every extra kilogram is extra load on every rep. This is real and worth acknowledging without judgement.</li><li><strong>Life load</strong> — a client working night shifts with two small children has different recovery capacity from a university student, and their program should reflect that.</li></ul><aside class=\"callout callout-coach-s-eye\"><p class=\"callout-label\">COACH&#x27;S EYE</p><p>The template is where you start, not what you deliver. The professional skill is adjustment — and the raw material for adjustment is information. Ask about sleep. Ask about work. Ask what hurt yesterday. A trainer who runs the same program on everyone is not coaching; they are distributing paperwork.</p></aside>",
"1.2-quiz": "<p>Five questions, ordered from straightforward to challenging.</p><ol><li>At which stage of the adaptation cycle does a trainee actually become stronger?</li></ol><ul class=\"options\"><li>A) Stimulus</li><li>B) Fatigue</li><li>C) Recovery and adaptation</li><li>D) During the final repetition of a set</li></ul><ol start=\"2\"><li>Which is NOT one of the six levers of progressive overload?</li></ol><ul class=\"options\"><li>A) Slower tempo</li><li>B) Harder leverage</li><li>C) Greater range of motion</li><li>D) Higher training frequency</li></ul><ol start=\"3\"><li>A pushup performed at tempo 3-1-1-0 means:</li></ol><ul class=\"options\"><li>A) 3 seconds up, 1 second pause at the top, 1 second down, no bottom pause</li><li>B) 3 seconds down, 1 second pause at the bottom, 1 second up, no top pause</li><li>C) 3 sets, 1 minute rest, 1 rep per second, no pause</li><li>D) 3 seconds of bracing, 1 rep, 1 second rest, no pause</li></ul><ol start=\"4\"><li>Because early progress is largely skill acquisition, beginners should:</li></ol><ul class=\"options\"><li>A) Train each pattern once a week with maximum volume</li><li>B) Train each pattern about three times a week for more practice repetitions</li><li>C) Train to failure on every set to maximise stimulus</li><li>D) Focus on isolation exercises before compound movements</li></ul><ol start=\"5\"><li>A client has stalled at 6 pull-ups for five weeks, trains five days a week, and sleeps five to six hours a night. Your first intervention should be to:</li></ol><ul class=\"options\"><li>A) Add a sixth training day</li><li>B) Switch to a different pull-up variation</li><li>C) Investigate recovery — sleep, stress, food — before changing the program</li><li>D) Increase volume by adding two sets</li></ul>",
"1.3.1": "<p>You do not need a medical degree. You need enough working anatomy to answer three questions, every time you watch someone move:</p><p><strong>What is working? What should be working? What is compensating?</strong></p><p>That third question is where coaching happens.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Muscle</strong></th><th><strong>Where</strong></th><th><strong>Job in calisthenics</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Pectoralis major</strong></td><td>Chest</td><td>Draws the upper arm across and in front of the body. Main engine of the pushup.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Anterior deltoid</strong></td><td>Front of shoulder</td><td>Raises the arm forward and overhead. Heavily involved in dips and all pressing.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Lateral deltoid</strong></td><td>Side of shoulder</td><td>Raises the arm out to the side; stabilises overhead.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Triceps brachii</strong></td><td>Back of upper arm</td><td>Straightens the elbow. Dominant in dips, close-grip pushing, and all lockouts.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Serratus anterior</strong></td><td>Side of ribcage, under the armpit</td><td>Pulls the shoulder blade forward and around the ribcage. Critical for healthy overhead work.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Rectus abdominis &amp;</strong> <strong>obliques</strong></td><td>Torso</td><td>Prevent the lower back from sagging in any plank-based push.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Gluteus maximus</strong></td><td>Buttock</td><td>Extends the hip. Drives squats and jumps.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Quadriceps</strong></td><td>Front of thigh</td><td>Straightens the knee.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Gastrocnemius &amp; soleus</strong></td><td>Calf</td><td>Push off through the foot.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><strong>The one nobody knows about: serratus anterior.</strong> It runs along the side of your ribs and pulls the shoulder blade forward and upward around the ribcage. When it is weak or asleep, the shoulder blade cannot rotate properly as the arm goes overhead, which narrows the space in the shoulder joint and leads directly to impingement. It is trained by the scapular pushup, and it is the reason that unglamorous little drill appears in every warm-up in this manual.</p><aside class=\"callout callout-coach-s-eye\"><p class=\"callout-label\">COACH&#x27;S EYE</p><p>Most beginners push almost entirely with pecs and front delts, leaving triceps and serratus offline. The visible signature: elbows flare out wide, and the shoulder blades never move at all through the whole rep. Module 1.7 fixes this in about three weeks.</p></aside>",
"1.3.2": "<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Muscle</strong></th><th><strong>Where</strong></th><th><strong>Job in calisthenics</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Latissimus dorsi</strong></td><td>Broad muscle of the back</td><td>Pulls the arm down and toward the body. The engine of the pull-up.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Biceps brachii</strong></td><td>Front of upper arm</td><td>Bends the elbow and turns the palm up.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Brachialis</strong></td><td>Under the biceps</td><td>Pure elbow flexion; strongest in neutral grip.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Forearm flexors</strong></td><td>Forearm</td><td>Grip. The bottleneck more often than people realise.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Trapezius (upper)</strong></td><td>Neck to shoulder</td><td>Elevates the shoulder blade. Usually overactive.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Trapezius (mid &amp; lower)</strong></td><td>Mid-back</td><td>Retracts and depresses the shoulder blade. Usually underactive.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Rhomboids</strong></td><td>Between the shoulder blades</td><td>Draw the blades toward the spine.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Posterior deltoid</strong></td><td>Back of shoulder</td><td>Draws the arm backward.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Abdominals</strong></td><td>Torso</td><td>Prevent excessive arching when hanging.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Hamstrings &amp; glutes</strong></td><td>Back of thigh and hip</td><td>Hip extension; draw the leg backward.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><strong>The imbalance you will see in almost every new client:</strong> overactive upper traps, underactive mid and lower traps. Modern life — desks, phones, driving — trains the shrug and neglects the pull-down. The visible signature is shoulders sitting high and rolled forward. This is why scapular depression work appears in every single warm-up from here on.</p><aside class=\"callout callout-coach-s-eye\"><p class=\"callout-label\">COACH&#x27;S EYE</p><p>The two most common pulling faults come from the same root. A trainee who pulls with biceps instead of lats bends the elbows <em>before</em> the shoulder blades move. A trainee with no scapular control hangs with shoulders shrugged to their ears and generates almost no lat force at all. Both are fixed by the same instruction: <strong>make the shoulder blade move first.</strong></p></aside>",
"1.3.3": "<p>Every movement has muscles producing motion and muscles holding everything else still. In calisthenics, the stabilisers matter unusually much, because the entire body is the load.</p><p>Take the pushup: - <strong>Prime movers:</strong> pecs, anterior delts, triceps. - <strong>Stabilisers:</strong> abdominals, obliques, glutes, quads, serratus.</p><p>If the stabilisers quit, the hips sag, the lower back over-extends, and the movement becomes both less effective and more risky — <strong>even though the prime movers could easily have completed the rep.</strong></p><p>This gives you the single most useful diagnostic question in Stage 1:</p><p class=\"rule\"><strong>When a rep looks wrong, ask: is the prime mover too weak, or did the stabilisers fail and the</strong> <strong>body find a workaround?</strong></p><p>The two answers lead to completely different interventions.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Problem</strong></th><th><strong>Diagnosis</strong></th><th><strong>Intervention</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Pushup with sagging hips from rep 1</td><td>Stabiliser failure — core never braced</td><td>Teach the plank and the brace. Do not regress the push.</td></tr><tr><td>Pushup where reps 1–5 are clean and rep 6 sags</td><td>Prime mover fatigue</td><td>Stop the set at 5. Program 3×5 and build.</td></tr><tr><td>Pull-up with wild kipping from rep 1</td><td>Prime mover too weak for the load</td><td>Regress: negatives or band assistance.</td></tr><tr><td>Pull-up clean at first, swinging on the last rep</td><td>Prime mover fatigue</td><td>End the set one rep earlier.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Notice the pattern: <strong>when the fault appears from the first rep, it is usually a capability or control problem.</strong> <strong>When it appears at the end of a set, it is fatigue, and the fix is simply a shorter set.</strong></p><aside class=\"callout callout-from-the-floor\"><p class=\"callout-label\">FROM THE FLOOR</p><p>A client&#x27;s squat looks fine for eight reps, then their knees start caving inward. They ask if they need stronger glutes. Read the timing. Reps 1–8 were fine, so the glutes are strong enough to do the movement — they just cannot do it <em>twelve</em> times yet. This is fatigue, not weakness. Program sets of eight, add reps gradually, and the problem disappears. If the knees had caved on rep one, the answer would be different.</p></aside>",
"1.3.4": "<p>Muscle is not the only tissue you are training, and it is not the slowest.</p><ul><li><strong>Muscle</strong> responds quickly to training — noticeable changes within weeks.</li><li><strong>Tendons and ligaments</strong> have far less blood supply and adapt much more slowly — <strong>months</strong>, not weeks.</li></ul><p>This single fact explains most calisthenics injuries.</p><p>A motivated trainee builds muscular strength fast enough to attempt movements their connective tissue has not yet caught up with. The muscle can produce the force. The tendon cannot yet tolerate it, particularly at extreme leverages. The result is the classic calisthenics injury list: elbow tendinopathy from rushing straight-arm work, wrist pain from too much handstand volume too early, front-of-shoulder pain from deep dips before the joint was ready.</p><aside class=\"callout callout-myth-vs-reality\"><p class=\"callout-label\">MYTH VS REALITY</p><p><strong>&quot;If I can hold the position, I&#x27;m ready to train it.&quot;</strong> Reality: momentary capability is not tissue tolerance. You might hold a tuck front lever for five seconds today. Doing twenty sets of it weekly for a month is an entirely different demand, and your elbows get a vote. Add volume to any straight-arm or extreme-leverage work slowly — this is precisely why levers and planche belong in Stage 2 and 3, not Stage 1.</p></aside><p>Practical rules that come from this:</p><ol><li><strong>Progress leverage slower than your muscles want.</strong> If it feels like you could move up two steps, move up one.</li><li><strong>Straight-arm work is a separate category.</strong> Anything loading the elbow with a locked arm — levers, planche, elbow lever — needs its own slow, dedicated build-up.</li><li><strong>Condition the wrists before loading them.</strong> Ten minutes a week of wrist prep in Stage 1 prevents months of handstand problems in Stage 2.</li><li><strong>Joint pain is information, not weakness.</strong> Muscular burn is fine. Sharp or pinching joint pain means stop, now.</li></ol>",
"1.3.5": "<ul><li><strong>Closed chain:</strong> the working limb is fixed against something immovable. Pushups, pull-ups, squats.</li><li><strong>Open chain:</strong> the limb moves freely through space. A dumbbell curl, a leg extension.</li></ul><p>Almost all calisthenics is closed chain, and this is a genuine advantage. Closed-chain movement recruits stabilisers heavily, distributes force across multiple joints instead of concentrating it at one, and loads joints in a pattern that generally resembles real-life demands. It is a good answer to have ready when a client asks why you are not handing them a machine.</p>",
"1.3.6": "<p>Three planes. Useful shorthand for spotting programming gaps.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Plane</strong></th><th><strong>Movement direction</strong></th><th><strong>Calisthenics examples</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Sagittal</strong></td><td>Forward and backward</td><td>Pushups, pull-ups, squats, dips — the overwhelming majority</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Frontal</strong></td><td>Side to side</td><td>Side plank, human flag, lateral lunge, archer variations</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Transverse</strong></td><td>Rotation</td><td>Russian twists, windshield wipers, twisting leg raises</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Notice how lopsided that table is. Calisthenics is overwhelmingly sagittal. A complete program deliberately imports some frontal and transverse work — side planks and anti-rotation core work in Stage 1, archer variations and human flag progressions later. A body that has only ever been loaded forward and backward is not a robust body.</p>",
"1.3-quiz": "<p>Five questions, ordered from straightforward to challenging.</p><ol><li>Which muscle pulls the shoulder blade forward and around the ribcage, and is critical for safe overhead work?</li></ol><ul class=\"options\"><li>A) Rhomboids</li><li>B) Serratus anterior</li><li>C) Latissimus dorsi</li><li>D) Posterior deltoid</li></ul><ol start=\"2\"><li>Tendons and ligaments adapt to training:</li></ol><ul class=\"options\"><li>A) Faster than muscle, due to higher blood supply</li><li>B) At approximately the same rate as muscle</li><li>C) Much more slowly than muscle, over months rather than weeks</li><li>D) Only in response to external load, not bodyweight</li></ul><ol start=\"3\"><li>In a pushup, the abdominals, glutes, quadriceps, and serratus function as:</li></ol><ul class=\"options\"><li>A) Prime movers</li><li>B) Antagonists</li><li>C) Stabilisers</li><li>D) Synergists to the triceps</li></ul><ol start=\"4\"><li>A client&#x27;s hips sag from the very first rep of a pushup. This indicates:</li></ol><ul class=\"options\"><li>A) Prime mover weakness — regress the pushup</li><li>B) A stabiliser or bracing problem — teach the plank and the brace</li><li>C) Poor shoulder mobility</li><li>D) Insufficient warm-up</li></ul><ol start=\"5\"><li>The same client&#x27;s pushups are clean for six reps, then the hips sag on the seventh. The correct response is:</li></ol><ul class=\"options\"><li>A) Regress to knee pushups</li><li>B) Add core exercises to the warm-up</li><li>C) Program sets of five or six — this is fatigue, not weakness</li><li>D) Cue harder bracing and continue to twelve reps</li></ul>",
"1.4.1": "<p>Imagine a mechanic who fits new tyres without ever checking the alignment. The tyres are fine. They will also wear out sideways in four months.</p><p>Loading a body without screening it is the same error. A client with a locked-up thoracic spine who is put straight into overhead pressing will get their range from somewhere — usually by arching the lower back or shrugging the shoulders. They will complete the exercise. They will also be compensating on every single rep, and compensation repeated a thousand times becomes an injury.</p><p>Screening takes ten minutes. It tells you where to start, what to avoid, and what to work on alongside the main program.</p><aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p><strong>A boundary, stated once and meant permanently:</strong> screening is not diagnosis. You are looking for <em>movement restrictions</em> to inform exercise selection. You are not identifying pathology, naming conditions, or treating anything. The moment you find pain rather than restriction, you are outside your scope and the answer is a referral. Module 1.13 covers this in full.</p></aside>",
"1.4.2": "<p>Before anyone moves, ask. Five minutes, and it changes everything downstream.</p><p><strong>Training history</strong></p><ul><li>Have you trained before? What, how long, how recently?</li><li>Any sports background? (A former gymnast and a lifelong non-exerciser are different animals.)</li><li>What has worked for you before? What did you hate?</li></ul><p><strong>Injury and medical history</strong></p><ul><li>Any current pain, anywhere? Where, when, how long?</li><li>Past injuries, surgeries, dislocations, fractures?</li><li>Anything a doctor has told you to avoid?</li><li>Any condition I should know about before we load your body?</li></ul><p><strong>Lifestyle and recovery</strong></p><ul><li>How many hours do you sleep, typically?</li><li>How physically demanding is your job? Sitting all day, or on your feet?</li><li>How much stress are you under right now?</li><li>How many days a week can you <em>realistically</em> train? (Not aspirationally. Realistically.)</li></ul><p><strong>Goals</strong></p><ul><li>What do you actually want? Push for specifics.</li><li>Any deadline? Wedding, competition, holiday?</li><li>What would make you feel this was worth the money in three months?</li></ul><aside class=\"callout callout-coach-s-eye\"><p class=\"callout-label\">COACH&#x27;S EYE</p><p>When a client says &quot;I want to get fit,&quot; that is not a goal, it is a mood. Keep asking. &quot;Fit for what?&quot; Underneath, you will usually find something concrete and often emotional — they want to play with their kids without getting winded, they want to stop feeling weak next to their friends, they want to prove something to themselves. Find that, and you have the thing that will keep them showing up in week nine when motivation is gone. This is genuinely more valuable than any programming decision you will make.</p></aside><p><strong>Red flags requiring medical clearance before you begin:</strong> chest pain or pressure with exertion, dizziness or fainting, uncontrolled blood pressure, recent surgery, pregnancy or recent postpartum, any bone or joint problem that exercise makes worse, or any medication affecting balance or heart rate. When in doubt: clearance first. You are not qualified to make that call, and you do not want to be the one who got it wrong.</p>",
"1.4.3": "<p>Run these in order. Each takes about a minute. Record what you find.</p><h4><strong>Screen 1 — Overhead reach</strong></h4><p><em>What it checks:</em> shoulder flexion and thoracic extension — the prerequisite for all overhead pushing and hanging.</p><p><em>How:</em> Stand with back against a wall, feet a few inches out, lower back flat against the wall. Raise straight arms overhead and try to touch the wall with the backs of the hands, without arching the lower back off the wall.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Result</strong></th><th><strong>Meaning</strong></th><th><strong>Action</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Hands touch, back stays flat</td><td>Full overhead range</td><td>Cleared for overhead work</td></tr><tr><td>Hands touch only by arching</td><td>Range is borrowed from the spine</td><td>Wall slides, thoracic mobility; limit overhead volume</td></tr><tr><td>Hands do not reach</td><td>Significant restriction</td><td>Build overhead range before pike pushups; use dips instead</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4><strong>Screen 2 — Hang test</strong></h4><p><em>What it checks:</em> shoulder tolerance of a loaded overhead position, and grip.</p><p><em>How:</em> Hang from a bar with straight arms for 30 seconds. Observe.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Result</strong></th><th><strong>Action</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>30 seconds, comfortable, shoulders not at ears</td><td>Cleared for pulling progressions</td></tr><tr><td>Under 15 seconds, grip fails</td><td>Grip is the bottleneck — Module 1.9</td></tr><tr><td>Shoulders shrug to ears, cannot depress</td><td>Scapular control first — Module 1.7</td></tr><tr><td>Any pain</td><td>Stop. Do not load. Refer.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4><strong>Screen 3 — Deep squat</strong></h4><p><em>What it checks:</em> ankle, knee, hip, and spine working together.</p><p><em>How:</em> Feet shoulder width, arms forward, squat as deep as possible while keeping heels on the floor and spine neutral.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Result</strong></th><th><strong>Meaning</strong></th><th><strong>Action</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Thighs below parallel, heels down, back neutral</td><td>Full squat pattern</td><td>Cleared for full squats</td></tr><tr><td>Heels lift</td><td>Ankle restriction</td><td>Elevate heels; ankle mobility work</td></tr><tr><td>Lower back rounds at the bottom</td><td>Hip restriction or depth beyond control</td><td>Box squat to the depth they own</td></tr><tr><td>Knees collapse inward</td><td>Motor control or glute weakness</td><td>Band around knees; cue &quot;knees out&quot;</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4><strong>Screen 4 — Wall shoulder flexion, arms only</strong></h4><p><em>What it checks:</em> whether overhead restriction is coming from the shoulder itself or from the ribcage and spine.</p><p><em>How:</em> Lie on your back, knees bent, lower back flat on the floor. Raise straight arms overhead toward the floor behind you without the ribs flaring or the back arching.</p><p>If range is much better lying down than standing at the wall, the restriction is largely spinal, and thoracic mobility work is the priority. If it is equally limited in both, the shoulder itself needs attention.</p><h4><strong>Screen 5 — Plank hold</strong></h4><p><em>What it checks:</em> baseline trunk stability.</p><p><em>How:</em> Forearm plank. Time how long the position is held <em>well</em> — not how long they stay up.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Result</strong></th><th><strong>Action</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>45+ seconds, clean</td><td>Cleared for standard core work</td></tr><tr><td>20–45 seconds</td><td>Start here, build toward 45</td></tr><tr><td>Under 20 seconds, or hips sag immediately</td><td>Core is the limiter; regress push and hang volume</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4><strong>Screen 6 — Wrist extension</strong></h4><p><em>What it checks:</em> tolerance for floor and bar loading. Skipped by almost everyone and the cause of enormous frustration later.</p><p><em>How:</em> Kneel, palms flat on the floor, fingers pointing forward, and gently shift weight over the hands.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Result</strong></th><th><strong>Action</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Comfortable at roughly 90°</td><td>Cleared for floor pushups</td></tr><tr><td>Tight or uncomfortable</td><td>Wrist prep (Module 1.5); use parallettes or fists</td></tr><tr><td>Painful</td><td>Elevate hands; wrist work only; refer if it persists</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4><strong>Screen 7 — Single-leg balance</strong></h4><p><em>What it checks:</em> ankle stability and side-to-side asymmetry.</p><p><em>How:</em> Stand on one leg, 30 seconds, then the other. Then repeat with eyes closed if the first part is easy.</p><p>A big difference between sides is worth noting — it usually reflects an old ankle injury and predicts which side will struggle in split squats and pistol progressions.</p>",
"1.4.4": "<p>Once cleared, get numbers. Not to judge, but so that week twelve has something to be compared against. Clients forget how far they have come with remarkable speed; your log is what proves it to them.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Test</strong></th><th><strong>Record</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Max pushups, full range, good form</td><td>reps</td></tr><tr><td>Max inverted rows at a set bar height</td><td>reps</td></tr><tr><td>Max pull-ups (or longest dead hang if zero)</td><td>reps / seconds</td></tr><tr><td>Max bodyweight squats</td><td>reps</td></tr><tr><td>Plank hold, good form</td><td>seconds</td></tr><tr><td>Dead hang</td><td>seconds</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><strong>Stop each test when form breaks, not when the client collapses.</strong> A max test is a measurement of <em>quality</em> reps. Record the number and the ladder step they are at, and repeat every eight to twelve weeks.</p><aside class=\"callout callout-from-the-floor\"><p class=\"callout-label\">FROM THE FLOOR</p><p>A 45-year-old office worker, no training history, wants to start. Your screens find: hands don&#x27;t reach the wall overhead, heels lift in a deep squat, plank holds for 18 seconds, wrists tight. He asks when he can start pull-ups and handstands.</p><p>He starts today — just not with those. His program: incline pushups on parallettes (wrists), inverted rows at chest height, box squats to the depth he owns, plank progressions, plus wall slides and ankle mobility every session. Dips instead of overhead pressing until the overhead screen clears.</p><p>And here is the important part, the part that is actually a communication skill rather than a programming one: you tell him the plan <em>and</em> <em>the reason</em>, and you give him a timeline. &quot;Your shoulders can&#x27;t get overhead safely yet, so we&#x27;re building that for six to eight weeks while you get strong everywhere else. Then we add the overhead work.&quot; A client who understands why they are not doing pull-ups yet will wait patiently. A client who just feels held back will go and try them alone on Sunday and hurt themselves.</p></aside>",
"1.4-practical": "<ol><li>Run the full seven-screen assessment on another person. Record every result in writing.</li><li>From those results, write a complete list of exercises you would <strong>avoid</strong> for the first six weeks, with a reason for each.</li><li>Take the same person&#x27;s baseline test numbers.</li><li>Write out the intake conversation you would have with them — the actual questions, in order.</li><li><strong>Trainer track:</strong> film yourself explaining one restriction you found and your plan for it, in language a complete beginner would follow. Under two minutes.</li></ol>",
"1.5.1": "<p>Ask ten people why they warm up and nine will say &quot;to prevent injury.&quot; That is the least well-supported reason on the list.</p><p>Here is what a warm-up genuinely does:</p><ol><li><strong>Raises tissue temperature.</strong> Warm muscle produces more force and tolerates stretch better. This is real and measurable.</li><li><strong>Lubricates joints.</strong> Movement increases synovial fluid distribution, which matters enormously for the shoulder and wrist under calisthenics loads.</li><li><strong>Primes the nervous system.</strong> Rehearsing the pattern you are about to load makes the first working set feel like the third.</li><li><strong>Reveals today&#x27;s body.</strong> This is the underrated one. During a warm-up you find out that the left shoulder is tight today, or that sleep was bad, or that yesterday&#x27;s session is still present. That information changes what you should do in the next hour.</li><li><strong>Creates a transition.</strong> A psychological doorway from the rest of life into training. Do not dismiss this — for many clients it is the difference between a distracted session and a focused one.</li></ol><aside class=\"callout callout-myth-vs-reality\"><p class=\"callout-label\">MYTH VS REALITY</p><p><strong>&quot;Static stretching before training prevents injury.&quot;</strong> Reality: long static holds on cold tissue temporarily <em>reduce</em> force output and have never demonstrated a clear injury-prevention effect. They are not evil — they are just in the wrong place. Put the long holds after training, or in a separate session. Before training, move.</p></aside>",
"1.5.2": "<p><strong>Layer 1: General warm-up (2–3 minutes).</strong> Raise the heart rate and temperature. Brisk walking, skipping, light jogging, jumping jacks, arm swings. Simple aerobic movement. The goal is warm and slightly breathing harder — not tired.</p><p><strong>Layer 2: Joint preparation (3–4 minutes).</strong> Controlled circles and articular movement through every joint you are about to load: wrists, elbows, shoulders, spine, hips, knees, ankles. Slow, deliberate, exploring range rather than forcing it.</p><p><strong>Layer 3: Movement-specific mobility (3–4 minutes).</strong> Address the specific restrictions this body has, and open the specific positions today&#x27;s session demands. Overhead work today? Wall slides. Squats today? Ankle rockers and deep squat holds.</p><p><strong>Layer 4: Pattern rehearsal (2–3 minutes).</strong> Easier versions of today&#x27;s actual movements. Scapular pull-ups before pull-ups. Incline pushups before floor pushups. A few bodyweight squats before loaded squats.</p><p><strong>Total: 10–14 minutes.</strong> Non-negotiable in Stage 1. It will feel long for the first fortnight and then it will feel obviously necessary.</p>",
"1.5.3": "<p>Read this section twice. Wrist problems are the single most common reason calisthenics trainees stop progressing, and they are almost entirely preventable.</p><p>The wrist was not designed to be a weight-bearing joint. In calisthenics we ask it to carry substantial load in extreme extension — pushups, and eventually handstands where it takes the entire bodyweight. Unprepared wrists develop pain that takes months to resolve.</p><p>Four minutes, every session:</p><ol><li>Wrist circles.</strong> Fingers interlaced, circle 10 times each direction.</li><li>Palm rocks.</strong> Kneel, palms flat, fingers forward. Rock weight gently forward over the hands and back. 10 slow reps. This is the primary wrist-extension drill.</li><li>Fingers-back rocks.</strong> Palms flat, fingers pointing <em>back</em> toward your knees. Rock gently. 10 reps. Uncomfortable at first — go carefully and never into pain.</li><li>Back-of-hand rocks.</strong> Backs of hands on the floor, fingers pointing toward each other, rock gently. 8 reps. Trains wrist flexion, the neglected direction.</li><li>Fist support.</strong> Make fists, knuckles down, wrists straight, hold 20–30 seconds. Builds the neutral-wrist position and a useful alternative for anyone with wrist pain.</li><li>Finger extensions.</strong> Press fingertips into the floor and lift the palm. 10 reps. Strengthens the extensors that oppose all the gripping.</li></ol><aside class=\"callout callout-coach-s-eye\"><p class=\"callout-label\">COACH&#x27;S EYE</p><p>Wrist pain in a beginner is almost always volume, not weakness. They did 200 pushups in their first week because they were excited. Cut volume, add the protocol above, elevate the hands on parallettes or push from fists, and it usually resolves in two weeks. What does not work is training through it.</p></aside>",
"1.5.4": "<p>The most loaded and most vulnerable joint in this sport. Full detail in Module 1.7, but every warm-up needs:</p><ol><li>Arm circles.</strong> Small to large, 10 each direction.</li><li>Band or towel pull-aparts.</strong> Arms straight, pull apart with shoulder blades, 15 reps. Wakes up the chronically underactive mid-back.</li><li>Wall slides.</strong> Back to wall, forearms on wall, elbows at shoulder height. Slide arms overhead maintaining contact, without the lower back arching off the wall. 10 reps. The single best overhead-range drill for desk-bound bodies.</li><li>Scapular pull-ups.</strong> Hang, and without bending the elbows at all, pull the shoulder blades down so the chest rises slightly. 8 reps. Every session, forever.</li><li>Scapular pushups.</strong> Top of pushup, arms locked, sink the chest between the blades then push the upper back toward the ceiling. 10 reps. Wakes up the serratus.</li></ol>",
"1.5.5": "<p><strong>Hips</strong></p><ul><li><strong>90/90 transitions</strong> — seated, both knees bent at 90°, rotate side to side. 8 each side. Opens internal and external rotation.</li><li><strong>Hip circles</strong> on all fours, 8 each direction each leg.</li><li><strong>Deep squat hold</strong> — sit in the deepest squat you can hold, 30–45 seconds. Hold onto something if needed. The most efficient hip-opener in existence for squat depth.</li></ul><p><strong>Ankles</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Ankle rockers</strong> — half-kneeling, drive the front knee forward over the toes keeping the heel down. 10 each side. Directly fixes the lifting-heels squat fault.</li><li><strong>Calf raises and lowers</strong>, 15 reps.</li></ul><p><strong>Spine</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cat-cow</strong>, 10 slow reps. Segmental spinal movement.</li><li><strong>Thoracic rotations</strong> on all fours — hand behind head, rotate elbow up toward the ceiling. 8 each side. Addresses the transverse-plane gap from Module 1.3.</li><li><strong>Hip flexor stretch with reach</strong>, 30 seconds each side. Counters sitting.</li></ul>",
"1.5.6": "<p>Six to eight minutes. Two jobs: bring the nervous system down from a sympathetic state, and use the post-training window when tissue is warm and receptive for the long static holds you were told not to do earlier.</p><ol><li>Two minutes of easy movement until breathing normalises.</li><li>Chest and front-shoulder stretch — doorway or wall, 30 seconds each side.</li><li>Lat stretch — hanging or overhead reach, 30 seconds each side.</li><li>Hip flexor stretch, 30 seconds each side.</li><li>Hamstring stretch, 30 seconds each side.</li><li>Forearm and wrist stretch, 20 seconds each direction.</li><li>Two to three minutes of slow nasal breathing — longer exhale than inhale.</li></ol><p>That last item looks like filler and is not. Training is a sympathetic (fight-or-flight) stimulus. Recovery happens in the parasympathetic state. Two minutes of deliberate slow breathing accelerates that shift, and it is free.</p><aside class=\"callout callout-try-this-now\"><p class=\"callout-label\">TRY THIS NOW</p><p>Sit down. Breathe in through your nose for 4 seconds, out for 8. Do it six times. Notice your heart rate and your shoulders. That is a nervous-system switch you can operate voluntarily, and most of your future clients do not know they have it.</p></aside>",
"1.5.7": "<p>Your session-ready template. Twelve minutes.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Layer</strong></th><th><strong>Drill</strong></th><th><strong>Volume</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>General</td><td>Brisk walk, skipping, or jumping jacks</td><td>2 min</td></tr><tr><td>Joints</td><td>Wrist protocol (all six drills)</td><td>4 min</td></tr><tr><td>Joints</td><td>Arm circles, cat-cow, hip circles, ankle rockers</td><td>2 min</td></tr><tr><td>Specific</td><td>Band pull-aparts</td><td>15</td></tr><tr><td>Specific</td><td>Wall slides</td><td>10</td></tr><tr><td>Specific</td><td>Scapular pull-ups</td><td>2 × 8</td></tr><tr><td>Specific</td><td>Scapular pushups</td><td>2 × 10</td></tr><tr><td>Rehearsal</td><td>Bodyweight squats</td><td>10</td></tr><tr><td>Rehearsal</td><td>Easy version of today&#x27;s main push and pull</td><td>5–8 each</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",
"1.5-quiz": "<p>Five questions, ordered from straightforward to challenging.</p><ol><li>Long static stretches before training:</li></ol><ul class=\"options\"><li>A) Reliably prevent injury</li><li>B) Temporarily reduce force output and have not demonstrated a clear injury-prevention effect</li><li>C) Should be held for at least 90 seconds each</li><li>D) Are essential before any overhead work</li></ul><ol start=\"2\"><li>The palm rock drill — kneeling with palms flat and fingers forward, rocking weight over the hands — primarily trains:</li></ol><ul class=\"options\"><li>A) Wrist flexion</li><li>B) Grip strength</li><li>C) Wrist extension</li><li>D) Finger extensor strength</li></ul><ol start=\"3\"><li>Which is the <em>weakest</em> justification for warming up?</li></ol><ul class=\"options\"><li>A) Raising tissue temperature</li><li>B) Preventing injury</li><li>C) Priming the nervous system</li><li>D) Revealing how the body is that day</li></ul><ol start=\"4\"><li>Ankle rockers in a warm-up most directly fix which squat fault?</li></ol><ul class=\"options\"><li>A) Knees collapsing inward</li><li>B) Lower back rounding at the bottom</li><li>C) Heels lifting off the floor</li><li>D) Chest dropping forward</li></ul><ol start=\"5\"><li>A client reports wrist pain in week two after an enthusiastic first week. The correct intervention is:</li></ol><ul class=\"options\"><li>A) Rest completely for six weeks</li><li>B) Cut volume, add the wrist protocol, and elevate the hands on parallettes or push from fists</li><li>C) Continue training and add a wrist brace</li><li>D) Refer immediately for imaging</li></ul>",
"1.6.1": "<p>Seven patterns. Own these and you have the vocabulary for every skill in Stages 2 and 3 — the muscle-up, the front lever, the handstand, the planche. All of them are recombinations of what is in this module.</p><p>Each movement below gives you five things:</p><ul><li><strong>Why it matters</strong> — where it leads</li><li><strong>The progression ladder</strong> — with numeric targets, so nobody has to guess when to advance</li><li><strong>Cues</strong> — the words that actually change what a body does</li><li><strong>Common faults</strong> — with cause and fix</li><li><strong>Programming notes</strong> — where it sits in a session</li></ul><p>A note on the targets: they are <em>minimums with good form</em>, not personal bests. Three sets of twelve where the twelfth rep looks like the first. If the last rep is ugly, you have not hit the target, whatever the number says.</p><h4><strong>Why it matters</strong></h4><p>The pushup is the most useful strength exercise in existence, and the most commonly performed badly. It is a moving plank: it trains the chest, shoulders, and triceps while simultaneously demanding that the entire trunk hold a rigid line. That dual demand is what makes it a foundation.</p><p>It leads to: dips, pseudo-planche pushups, handstand pushups, and eventually the planche itself. Every one of those is a pushup with the leverage changed.</p><h4><strong>The progression ladder</strong></h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Step</strong></th><th><strong>Movement</strong></th><th><strong>Target before advancing</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Wall pushup</td><td>3 × 15</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Incline pushup, hands at chest height</td><td>3 × 12</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Incline pushup, hands at knee height</td><td>3 × 12</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Knee pushup</td><td>3 × 12</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td><strong>Full pushup</strong></td><td>3 × 10</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Full pushup, 3-second eccentric</td><td>3 × 8</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Close-grip / diamond pushup</td><td>3 × 8</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Decline pushup, feet elevated</td><td>3 × 10</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Archer pushup</td><td>3 × 6 each side</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Pseudo-planche pushup</td><td>Stage 2</td></tr></tbody></table></div><aside class=\"callout callout-coach-s-eye\"><p class=\"callout-label\">COACH&#x27;S EYE</p><p>Notice that inclines come <em>before</em> knee pushups. This is deliberate and it is the opposite of how most people are taught.</p><p>A knee pushup breaks the body line. The trunk no longer has to hold a plank, so the trainee learns the arm movement without ever learning the bracing — and then has to un-learn it later when they reach full pushups and discover their hips sag.</p><p>An incline pushup keeps the line perfectly intact and reduces only the load. Same pattern, less weight, nothing to un-teach. Whenever you have the choice between reducing load and breaking position, <strong>reduce load.</strong> This principle applies far beyond pushups.</p></aside><h4><strong>Cues</strong></h4><ul><li>Hands just outside shoulder width, fingers spread wide, weight through the whole palm — not just the heel of the hand.</li><li><strong>&quot;Screw your hands into the floor.&quot;</strong> Attempt to rotate the palms outward without moving them. This externally rotates the shoulder, engages the lats, and stops elbow flare instantly. This is the highest-value pushup cue in existence.</li><li>Elbows travel back at roughly 45° to the torso — not straight out to the sides, not pinned to the ribs.</li><li>One straight line from heel to head. Squeeze glutes, brace the abdominals, and set that line <strong>before</strong> the first rep.</li><li>Lower until the chest is a fist&#x27;s height from the floor. Full range or the rep does not count.</li><li>At the top, push the floor away — let the shoulder blades wrap forward (protraction). Do not stop short.</li><li>Neck neutral. Eyes on a spot slightly ahead of the hands.</li><li>Breathe in on the way down, out on the way up.</li></ul><h4><strong>Common faults</strong></h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Fault</strong></th><th><strong>Cause</strong></th><th><strong>Fix</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Hips sagging from rep 1</td><td>Core never braced</td><td>Regress to plank holds; re-set the brace before each set</td></tr><tr><td>Hips sagging on final reps</td><td>Core fatigue</td><td>Shorten the set by two reps</td></tr><tr><td>Hips in the air, piked</td><td>Avoiding the hard part</td><td>Cue &quot;hips down, line straight&quot;; reduce reps</td></tr><tr><td>Head nods down, chest stays high</td><td>Faking range</td><td>Cue chest to touch a target</td></tr><tr><td>Elbows flared to 90°</td><td>No external rotation</td><td>&quot;Screw hands into floor&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>Partial range</td><td>Load too high</td><td>Regress one ladder step</td></tr><tr><td>Shoulders shrugging up</td><td>No serratus control</td><td>Scapular pushups; Module 1.7</td></tr><tr><td>Wrist pain</td><td>Volume or wrist extension</td><td>Parallettes, fists, wrist protocol</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4><strong>Programming notes</strong></h4><p>Sits early in the session, after the vertical pull. Three to four sets. Stage 1 rep range 8–12 for most trainees. A training- age beginner can do pushups three times a week comfortably — they recover fast.</p><aside class=\"callout callout-try-this-now\"><p class=\"callout-label\">TRY THIS NOW</p><p>Do a normal pushup. Then do one applying the &quot;screw your hands into the floor&quot; cue. Feel your armpits and the outside of your back switch on. Feel your elbows naturally come in. You just recruited your lats into a pushing exercise. Most people train pushups for years without discovering that.</p></aside>",
"1.6.2": "<h4><strong>Why it matters</strong></h4><p><strong>This is the most skipped and most important beginner movement in calisthenics.</strong> Everyone wants pull-ups. Almost nobody wants rows, because they are not impressive, they cannot be filmed, and nobody is going to be amazed at the park.</p><p>They are also the direct structural counterbalance to the pushup, the builder of the mid-back and rear shoulder that modern posture has neglected, and the movement that teaches scapular retraction under load — which every pulling skill in Stage 2 requires.</p><p>Also called the Australian pull-up or bodyweight row.</p><p>It leads to: pull-ups, front lever, and the horizontal pulling strength that keeps shoulders healthy for a lifetime of pushing.</p><h4><strong>The progression ladder</strong></h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Step</strong></th><th><strong>Movement</strong></th><th><strong>Target before advancing</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Standing band row, torso upright</td><td>3 × 15</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Bar at chest height, feet under the body</td><td>3 × 12</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Bar at waist height</td><td>3 × 12</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Bar at hip height, heels down, body straight</td><td>3 × 10</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Feet elevated row</td><td>3 × 10</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Feet elevated, wide or close grip</td><td>3 × 8</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Feet elevated, 3-second eccentric</td><td>3 × 8</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Archer row</td><td>3 × 6 each side</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>One-arm row progression</td><td>Stage 2</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4><strong>Cues</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Full body line.</strong> This is a plank that moves. Glutes squeezed, hips lifted, one line from heel to head.</li><li><strong>&quot;Shoulder blades first, then elbows.&quot;</strong> Say it in that order every single time. Retract the blades, <em>then</em> bend the arms.</li><li>Pull the <strong>chest</strong> to the bar, not the chin.</li><li>Elbows drive down and back past the ribs.</li><li>One-second pause at the top with the blades pinched together.</li><li>Lower for 2–3 seconds, with the blades releasing last.</li><li>Head stays neutral — do not crane the chin forward to reach.</li></ul><h4><strong>Common faults</strong></h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Fault</strong></th><th><strong>Cause</strong></th><th><strong>Fix</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Hips sagging</td><td>No glute or core brace</td><td>Raise the bar; re-cue the line</td></tr><tr><td>Elbows bend before blades move</td><td>Biceps-dominant pull</td><td>Slow the tempo; scapular-only pull reps</td></tr><tr><td>Chin reaches, chest doesn&#x27;t</td><td>Faking range</td><td>Place a target for the chest</td></tr><tr><td>Shrugged shoulders</td><td>No scapular depression</td><td>Module 1.7 drills</td></tr><tr><td>Bouncing off the bottom</td><td>Using momentum</td><td>Enforce a one-second pause at full extension</td></tr><tr><td>Heels sliding</td><td>Surface or no brace</td><td>Brace against something; press heels down</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4><strong>Programming notes</strong></h4><p>Sits paired with the horizontal push — often literally alternated with it. Three to four sets of 8–12.</p><p><strong>Stage 1 rule worth stating plainly: program at least as much horizontal pulling as horizontal pushing.</strong> Given that most clients arrive with rounded shoulders from a decade of desk work, a 2:1 pull-to-push ratio for the first few months is defensible and often the right call.</p><aside class=\"callout callout-from-the-floor\"><p class=\"callout-label\">FROM THE FLOOR</p><p>Your client says rows are boring and asks to swap them for more pushups. Here is the answer that works: &quot;Every pushup pulls your shoulders forward. Every row pulls them back. If we only do pushups, in a year your shoulders will sit rolled forward and your overhead work will start hurting — and then we have to stop training to fix it. Rows are what let you keep pushing for the next ten years.&quot; Frame the boring movement as the thing protecting the exciting movement. Clients accept this almost every time.</p></aside>",
"1.6.3": "<h4><strong>Why it matters</strong></h4><p>Two routes into overhead and vertical pressing, and they serve different purposes.</p><p><strong>Dips</strong> build raw pressing strength through a large range and are the closest bodyweight equivalent to a heavy press. <strong>Pike pushups</strong> build the genuinely overhead position that handstands and handstand pushups require.</p><p>Train both. Dips leads to: weighted dips, muscle-ups, ring dips. Pike pushups lead to: handstand pushups.</p><h4><strong>Dip progression ladder</strong></h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Step</strong></th><th><strong>Movement</strong></th><th><strong>Target before advancing</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Bench dip, feet on floor, knees bent</td><td>3 × 12</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Bench dip, legs extended</td><td>3 × 12</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td><strong>Parallel bar support hold</strong></td><td>3 × 30 sec</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Band-assisted parallel bar dip</td><td>3 × 8</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Negative dip, 5-second lower</td><td>3 × 5</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td><strong>Full parallel bar dip</strong></td><td>3 × 8</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Full dip, 3-second eccentric</td><td>3 × 8</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Weighted dip</td><td>Stage 2</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><strong>Step 3 is not optional and cannot be skipped.</strong> The support hold teaches the shoulder to stay depressed under compressive load. A trainee who dips without owning the support hold is loading a shrugged, unstable shoulder — which is how front-of-shoulder pain starts.</p><h4><strong>Pike pushup progression ladder</strong></h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Step</strong></th><th><strong>Movement</strong></th><th><strong>Target before advancing</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Incline pike pushup, hands elevated</td><td>3 × 10</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Floor pike pushup</td><td>3 × 8</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Feet-elevated pike pushup</td><td>3 × 8</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Feet elevated high, near-vertical torso</td><td>3 × 6</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Wall-assisted handstand pushup</td><td>Stage 2</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4><strong>Cues — dips</strong></h4><ul><li>Start in a <strong>locked-out support hold with the shoulder blades actively depressed.</strong> Shoulders down and away from the ears, and they stay there.</li><li>Slight forward lean of the torso is normal, healthy, and more chest-dominant. Upright is more triceps-dominant. Both are legitimate.</li><li>Lower until the upper arm is roughly parallel to the ground — about 90° at the elbow.</li><li>Elbows track backward, staying reasonably close to the ribs.</li><li>Legs together, glutes lightly engaged, ribs down — no swinging.</li><li>Press all the way to a locked-out support at the top.</li></ul><h4><strong>Cues — pike pushups</strong></h4><ul><li>Hips high, forming an inverted V. The more vertical the torso, the harder and the more shoulder-dominant.</li><li>Hands shoulder width or slightly wider.</li><li>Lower the <strong>crown of the head</strong> toward the floor between the hands, not forward of them.</li><li>Elbows travel back and slightly out — around 45°.</li><li>Actively press the floor away at the top and let the blades wrap upward.</li></ul><h4><strong>Safety note on dip depth</strong></h4><p>Going deeper than shoulders-below-elbows loads the front of the shoulder capsule heavily at end range. Some very experienced athletes train this deliberately. <strong>A Stage 1 trainee should not.</strong> Stop at parallel until the joint has many months of loading behind it.</p><p>If a client reports a pinch at the front of the shoulder during dips: reduce depth immediately, check their scapular control, and reduce volume. Do not push through it. Front-of-shoulder pain that gets trained through becomes a six- month problem.</p><h4><strong>Common faults</strong></h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Fault</strong></th><th><strong>Cause</strong></th><th><strong>Fix</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Shoulders shrug at the bottom</td><td>No depression under load</td><td>Regress to support holds</td></tr><tr><td>Half reps</td><td>Load too high</td><td>Band assistance or negatives</td></tr><tr><td>Body swinging</td><td>No core brace</td><td>Legs together, glutes on, ribs down</td></tr><tr><td>Front-of-shoulder pinch</td><td>Too deep, too soon, or poor scapular control</td><td>Reduce depth now; screen scapula</td></tr><tr><td>Head forward in pike pushup</td><td>Avoiding the vertical load</td><td>Cue crown of head between hands</td></tr><tr><td>Elbows flared wide</td><td>No external rotation</td><td>Cue elbows back, not out</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4><strong>Programming notes</strong></h4><p>Vertical push sits mid-session, after the horizontal work. Three to four sets of 8–12 for dips, 6–10 for pike pushups.</p><p><strong>Pair vertical push with vertical pull in the same session, always.</strong> Dips plus pull-ups is one of the great pairings in all of strength training.</p>",
"1.6.4": "<h4><strong>Why it matters</strong></h4><p>The signature movement of the discipline. Also the most rushed, the most ego-driven, and the most commonly butchered.</p><p>Here is a claim worth sitting with: <strong>a trainee who can do three ugly, kipping, half-range pull-ups is in a worse</strong> <strong>position than one who can do ten clean inverted rows and zero pull-ups.</strong> The second has a foundation. The first has a motor pattern that will have to be dismantled, and probably an accumulating shoulder problem.</p><p>It leads to: muscle-ups, front lever, weighted pull-ups, one-arm pull-up.</p><h4><strong>The progression ladder</strong></h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Step</strong></th><th><strong>Movement</strong></th><th><strong>Target before advancing</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Dead hang</td><td>3 × 30 sec</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Active hang / scapular pull-up</td><td>3 × 10</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Inverted row, feet elevated</td><td>3 × 10</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Band-assisted pull-up</td><td>3 × 8</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Negative pull-up, 5-second lower</td><td>3 × 5</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td><strong>Full pull-up</strong></td><td>3 × 5</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Full pull-up, wider or narrower grip</td><td>3 × 8</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Full pull-up, 3-second eccentric</td><td>3 × 6</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>L-sit pull-up / archer pull-up</td><td>Stage 2</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><strong>The first-pull-up protocol.</strong> When a client is at steps 4 and 5, the fastest route is a combination: - <strong>Negatives</strong> build the eccentric strength — jump or step to the top, lower for 5 seconds. 3–5 sets of 3–5 reps. - <strong>Band assistance</strong> lets them practise the full pattern with real timing. - <strong>Active hangs and rows</strong> build the scapular control and mid-back the movement needs. - Run all three, twice a week, for six to eight weeks. Most beginners get their first pull-up in that window.</p><h4><strong>Grip variations</strong></h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Grip</strong></th><th><strong>Hands</strong></th><th><strong>Emphasis</strong></th><th><strong>Beginner suitability</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Pull-up</td><td>Overhand, shoulder width+</td><td>Lats, mid-back</td><td>Standard</td></tr><tr><td>Chin-up</td><td>Underhand</td><td>Biceps, lower lats</td><td><strong>Usually easiest — start here</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Neutral</td><td>Palms facing each other</td><td>Lats, brachialis</td><td><strong>Most shoulder-friendly</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Wide</td><td>Well outside shoulders</td><td>Upper lats, higher shoulder demand</td><td>Not for beginners</td></tr><tr><td>Mixed</td><td>One over, one under</td><td>Asymmetrical loading</td><td>Use sparingly</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Start beginners on chin-ups or neutral grip. Wide grip is not a beginner tool and produces more shoulder complaints than it produces progress.</p><h4><strong>Cues</strong></h4><ul><li>Hang with <strong>straight arms and an engaged shoulder</strong> — not a loose, collapsed, shrugged hang.</li><li><strong>&quot;Shoulder blades down first.&quot;</strong> The chest rises slightly <em>before</em> the elbows bend at all.</li><li>Drive the elbows down toward the hip pockets, not backward.</li><li>Ribs down, legs together, glutes lightly on. Resist the urge to kick.</li><li>Chin clears the bar with the chest lifting toward it.</li><li>Lower all the way to straight arms. Every rep. No exceptions.</li><li>Exhale on the way up.</li></ul><h4><strong>Common faults</strong></h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Fault</strong></th><th><strong>Cause</strong></th><th><strong>Fix</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Kipping and swinging from rep 1</td><td>Strength deficit hidden by momentum</td><td>Regress to negatives; enforce a still body</td></tr><tr><td>Swinging only on final reps</td><td>Fatigue</td><td>End the set one rep earlier</td></tr><tr><td>Half reps at the top</td><td>Load too high</td><td>Band assistance or negatives</td></tr><tr><td>Never reaching full extension at the bottom</td><td>Avoiding the hardest portion</td><td>Cue a dead hang at the bottom of every rep</td></tr><tr><td>Shrugged shoulders at the start</td><td>No scapular control</td><td>Active hangs, every session</td></tr><tr><td>Chin craned over the bar</td><td>Faking range</td><td>Cue &quot;chest to bar&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>Elbows flaring wide</td><td>Grip too wide</td><td>Narrow the grip</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4><strong>Programming notes</strong></h4><p>Pull-ups go <strong>first</strong> in the session, when the nervous system is freshest — this is the highest-skill, highest-demand movement in a Stage 1 program and it deserves the best conditions.</p><p>Three to five sets. Rep range depends on level: 3–6 for someone new to full pull-ups, 6–10 once established. For a trainee with zero pull-ups, higher frequency helps — negatives and assisted work three times a week is fine and usually better.</p><aside class=\"callout callout-coach-s-eye\"><p class=\"callout-label\">COACH&#x27;S EYE</p><p>When a client kips, do not say &quot;stop swinging.&quot; They are not swinging by choice; they are swinging because their lats cannot do the job and their body is solving the problem the only way it can. Telling them to stop just means they will fail the rep and feel bad.</p><p>Instead, remove the reason to swing: reduce the load with a band, or switch to negatives. The swing disappears on its own. <strong>Most</strong> <strong>form faults are not discipline problems, they are load problems.</strong> Fix the load and the form tends to fix itself.</p></aside>",
"1.6.5": "<h4><strong>Why it matters</strong></h4><p>Calisthenics athletes are notorious for having strong upper bodies and neglected legs. It is a joke in the community and it is also a genuine problem — it limits jumping, sprinting, explosive freestyle work, and general robustness.</p><p>The squat is the foundation of lower-body training and the gateway to the pistol squat, which is the calisthenics leg skill.</p><h4><strong>The progression ladder</strong></h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Step</strong></th><th><strong>Movement</strong></th><th><strong>Target before advancing</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Sit-to-stand from a chair</td><td>3 × 15</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Assisted squat, holding support</td><td>3 × 15</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Box squat to a bench</td><td>3 × 15</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td><strong>Bodyweight squat, full depth</strong></td><td>3 × 20</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Split squat</td><td>3 × 12 each leg</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Bulgarian split squat, rear foot elevated</td><td>3 × 10 each leg</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Box pistol squat to a high box</td><td>3 × 8 each leg</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Assisted pistol squat, holding support</td><td>3 × 6 each leg</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td><strong>Pistol squat</strong></td><td>3 × 5 each leg</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Weighted or elevated pistol</td><td>Stage 2</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4><strong>Cues</strong></h4><ul><li>Feet roughly shoulder width, toes turned slightly out — <strong>then adjust to the individual.</strong> There is no single correct stance; hip anatomy differs substantially between people.</li><li>Weight spread across the whole foot: big toe, little toe, heel — a tripod.</li><li><strong>Knees track over the toes and are allowed to travel forward past them.</strong> This is normal, safe, and necessary for full depth. The old &quot;never let knees pass the toes&quot; rule is a misunderstanding.</li><li>Chest up, spine in its natural curve — neither flattened nor over-arched.</li><li>Descend until the hip crease is at or below the knee, <strong>if it can be done without the lower back rounding.</strong> Their mobility sets their depth, not your preference.</li><li>Drive up through the whole foot. Squeeze the glutes at the top.</li><li>Breathe in on the way down, out on the way up.</li></ul><h4><strong>Common faults</strong></h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Fault</strong></th><th><strong>Cause</strong></th><th><strong>Fix</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Heels lift</td><td>Ankle mobility restriction</td><td>Elevate heels slightly; ankle rockers in warm-up</td></tr><tr><td>Lower back rounds at the bottom</td><td>Depth exceeds current mobility</td><td>Reduce depth to the point of control; box squat</td></tr><tr><td>Knees collapse inward from rep 1</td><td>Motor control or glute weakness</td><td>Band around knees; cue &quot;knees out&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>Knees collapse only on final reps</td><td>Glute fatigue</td><td>Shorten the set</td></tr><tr><td>Chest drops forward</td><td>Weak trunk, or excessive depth</td><td>Box squat to teach the position</td></tr><tr><td>Weight on toes throughout</td><td>Balance or ankle issue</td><td>Cue the foot tripod; heel-elevated squats</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4><strong>Programming notes</strong></h4><p>Lower body goes later in the session — it is fatiguing and you want the upper-body skill work fresh. Three to four sets, 12–20 reps for bodyweight squats, 8–12 for single-leg work.</p><p>Single-leg work is the primary progression route for legs in calisthenics, since adding external load is often unavailable. Split squats and Bulgarian split squats are where most Stage 1 leg progress actually comes from.</p>",
"1.6.6": "<h4><strong>Why it matters</strong></h4><p>Read this section carefully, because this is the genuine hole in almost every calisthenics program on the internet.</p><p>The hinge is hip-dominant movement — bending at the hips with relatively little knee bend, loading the hamstrings, glutes, and spinal erectors. In the weight room it is the deadlift and the Romanian deadlift. In calisthenics it is... frequently nothing at all.</p><p>The consequence is a predictable body: strong quads, strong upper body, weak posterior chain. That combination shows up as hamstring strains in sprinting, a weak lower back under load, and reduced power in anything explosive.</p><p>It leads to: Nordic curls, single-leg hinges, and the posterior-chain resilience that supports everything else.</p><h4><strong>The progression ladder</strong></h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Step</strong></th><th><strong>Movement</strong></th><th><strong>Target before advancing</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Hip bridge, both feet down</td><td>3 × 15</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Hip bridge, feet elevated</td><td>3 × 15</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Single-leg hip bridge</td><td>3 × 12 each leg</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Bodyweight good morning</td><td>3 × 15</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Single-leg hinge, reaching for the floor</td><td>3 × 12 each leg</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Nordic curl negative, assisted</td><td>3 × 5</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Nordic curl negative, 5-second lower</td><td>3 × 5</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Full Nordic curl</td><td>Stage 2</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4><strong>Cues</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Hinge means hips move backward, not knees forward.</strong> This is the whole distinction from a squat, and the one most beginners cannot feel at first.</li><li>Spine stays neutral throughout — the movement happens entirely at the hip.</li><li>Feel the stretch in the hamstrings, not the lower back. If the lower back is working hard, the spine is moving when it should not be.</li><li>Hip bridge: drive through the heels, squeeze the glutes hard at the top, ribs down. Do <strong>not</strong> get extra height by arching the lower back.</li><li>Nordic negatives: resist the entire way down. The knees stay fixed; only the hips and torso travel.</li></ul><aside class=\"callout callout-try-this-now\"><p class=\"callout-label\">TRY THIS NOW</p><p>Stand up with your feet hip-width apart. Put one hand on your lower back and one on your stomach. Now push your hips straight backward, like you are closing a car door with your backside, letting your chest come forward — knees barely bending. Feel the back of your thighs load up. Then do a squat and notice how different it is. <strong>That is the hinge, and if you have never deliberately</strong> <strong>trained it, one half of your hips has been on holiday.</strong></p></aside><h4><strong>Common faults</strong></h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Fault</strong></th><th><strong>Cause</strong></th><th><strong>Fix</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Squatting instead of hinging</td><td>Pattern not learned</td><td>Wall-touch drill: stand a foot from a wall, push hips back to touch it</td></tr><tr><td>Lower back rounding</td><td>Range beyond control</td><td>Reduce range; brace the trunk</td></tr><tr><td>Lower back arching at the top of a bridge</td><td>Over-extending instead of using glutes</td><td>Ribs down; squeeze glutes, not spine</td></tr><tr><td>Hamstring cramping in Nordics</td><td>New pattern, high demand</td><td>Reduce range and volume; build slowly</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4><strong>Programming notes</strong></h4><p>Paired with squats in the lower-body block. Three sets of 12–15 for bridges, lower reps for Nordic work. Nordic curls are extremely demanding on the hamstrings — start with two sets and build very gradually, or you will have a client who cannot walk properly for four days.</p>",
"1.6.7": "<h4><strong>Why it matters</strong></h4><p>The core&#x27;s primary job in calisthenics is not to produce movement. It is to <strong>resist</strong> movement, so force can transfer between the upper and lower body without the trunk collapsing.</p><p>Every skill in this sport depends on it. A front lever is a hollow-body hold with the arms loaded. A handstand is a hollow body upside down. A muscle-up is impossible with a loose trunk. The core is not an aesthetic afterthought — it is the structural link.</p><h4><strong>The progression ladder</strong></h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Step</strong></th><th><strong>Movement</strong></th><th><strong>Target before advancing</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Forearm plank</td><td>3 × 45 sec</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Hollow body hold, knees bent, hands by sides</td><td>3 × 30 sec</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Hollow body hold, legs extended</td><td>3 × 30 sec</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Hollow body hold, arms overhead</td><td>3 × 30 sec</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Dead hang knee raise</td><td>3 × 12</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Hanging tuck-to-L raise</td><td>3 × 10</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Hanging straight-leg raise</td><td>3 × 8</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Tucked L-sit on parallettes</td><td>3 × 20 sec</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Full L-sit</td><td>Stage 2</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><strong>Plus, in every program, one anti-rotation and one lateral movement:</strong></p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Movement</strong></th><th><strong>Target</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Side plank</td><td>3 × 30 sec each side</td></tr><tr><td>Bird dog</td><td>3 × 10 each side</td></tr><tr><td>Dead bug</td><td>3 × 10 each side</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>These address the frontal and transverse planes from Module 1.3 that calisthenics otherwise ignores completely.</p><h4><strong>The hollow body — the master position</strong></h4><p class=\"rule\"><strong>The hollow body is the single most important position in calisthenics.</strong></p><p>Lower back flat against the floor, ribs pulled down toward the hips, pelvis slightly tucked, legs and shoulders lifted, body forming a shallow banana curve with the whole spine in contact or near-contact with the floor.</p><p>This exact position appears, in some form, in: - the handstand - the front lever - the planche - the top of a pull-up - a well-executed pushup - the muscle-up transition</p><p>Teach it properly in Stage 1 and every skill in Stage 2 becomes measurably easier. Skip it and every skill in Stage 2 becomes a fight against your own loose midsection.</p><h4><strong>Cues</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Plank:</strong> one line from heel to head. Glutes squeezed, shoulders directly over elbows, ribs pulled down, chin neutral. Actively push the floor away with the forearms.</li><li><strong>Hollow:</strong> lower back pressed flat into the floor — this is the whole drill. If the back lifts off, the position is too hard: bend the knees and bring them closer until it flattens.</li><li><strong>Hanging leg raises:</strong> no swing at all. Control the descent as much as the lift. If the body starts swinging, the set is finished.</li><li><strong>Side plank:</strong> hips stacked and lifted, body in one line, no sagging toward the floor.</li></ul><h4><strong>Common faults</strong></h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Fault</strong></th><th><strong>Cause</strong></th><th><strong>Fix</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Lower back arches off floor in hollow</td><td>Position exceeds current strength</td><td>Bend knees, bring them closer to the chest</td></tr><tr><td>Plank with sagging or piked hips</td><td>Loss of brace, or hold too long</td><td>Shorten the hold to the time they can hold it <em>well</em></td></tr><tr><td>Swinging leg raises</td><td>Momentum substituting for strength</td><td>Reduce range; pause at the bottom of each rep</td></tr><tr><td>Holding breath throughout</td><td>Confusing bracing with breath-holding</td><td>Module 1.10</td></tr><tr><td>Neck straining in hollow</td><td>Head lifted by neck instead of trunk</td><td>Look at knees; keep the neck long</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4><strong>Programming notes</strong></h4><p>Core work sits at the end of the session — it is a limiter you do not want fatigued during skill work. Three to four sets. Time-based for holds, rep-based for raises.</p><aside class=\"callout callout-coach-s-eye\"><p class=\"callout-label\">COACH&#x27;S EYE</p><p>A 90-second plank is not a sign of a strong core. It is usually a sign of a person who learned to endure discomfort in a sagging position. Thirty seconds of a <em>perfect</em> plank is worth more than two minutes of a mediocre one — and it transfers to actual skills, whereas the two- minute version transfers to nothing except being good at planks.</p><p>When a client proudly reports a long plank time, film them. They will almost always see something they did not know was happening.</p></aside>",
"1.6-practical": "<p>Demonstrate with clean, honest form — filmed from the side is best:</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Movement</strong></th><th><strong>Standard</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Pushups</td><td>10, full range, chest to fist height</td></tr><tr><td>Inverted rows</td><td>10, feet at hip height or above, chest to bar</td></tr><tr><td>Dips</td><td>8 full, or 30-sec support hold + 8 assisted</td></tr><tr><td>Pull-ups</td><td>3 full, dead hang to chin over bar, or 5 × 5-sec negatives</td></tr><tr><td>Squats</td><td>20, full depth, heels down</td></tr><tr><td>Hip bridges</td><td>15 single-leg each side</td></tr><tr><td>Plank</td><td>45 seconds</td></tr><tr><td>Hollow hold</td><td>30 seconds</td></tr><tr><td>Side plank</td><td>30 seconds each side</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><strong>Trainer track, additionally:</strong></p><ul><li>Film yourself coaching another person through three of these movements.</li><li>For each, identify at least one fault, name whether it is a capability problem or a fatigue problem, apply a correction, and explain your reasoning aloud.</li><li>Write out the full progression ladder for all seven patterns from memory.</li></ul>",
"1.7.1": "<p><strong>Read this module twice.</strong> More calisthenics injuries trace back to the content of these pages than to anything else in Stage 1. If you retain only one module from this manual, retain this one.</p><p>The shoulder is the most mobile joint in the human body. It can move through more range in more directions than anything else you own.</p><p>It pays for that freedom with instability. The hip is a deep socket — a ball sitting inside a cup. The shoulder is closer to a golf ball on a tee: the head of the arm bone sits against a shallow, almost flat surface, held there by muscles, tendons, and a ring of cartilage rather than by bone.</p><p>In calisthenics we then take this shallow, mobile, muscle-dependent joint and load it overhead, hanging, and eventually fully inverted — positions most adults have not put their shoulders in since childhood.</p><p>The protective factor is not strength. Plenty of strong people have bad shoulders. <strong>The protective factor is control.</strong></p>",
"1.7.2": "<p>Most people imagine the shoulder blade as a fixed plate on the back that the arm hangs from. It is not. It <strong>glides</strong> across the ribcage, and for the arm to function correctly, it has to move — in a coordinated relationship with the arm called scapulohumeral rhythm.</p><p>When the arm goes overhead, roughly two-thirds of the movement comes from the shoulder joint itself and one-third from the shoulder blade rotating upward on the ribcage. If the blade does not do its share, the arm still gets overhead — by borrowing range from somewhere else, usually the lower back or by jamming the joint into its own limits.</p><h4><strong>The four scapular movements</strong></h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Movement</strong></th><th><strong>What happens</strong></th><th><strong>Where it belongs</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Elevation</strong></td><td>Blades rise toward the ears (a shrug)</td><td>Almost nowhere in loaded calisthenics — usually a fault</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Depression</strong></td><td>Blades pull down away from the ears</td><td>Every hang, pull, dip, and support hold</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Protraction</strong></td><td>Blades slide forward around the ribcage</td><td>Top of a pushup, overhead press, planche</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Retraction</strong></td><td>Blades pull together toward the spine</td><td>Start of every pull, rows, levers</td></tr></tbody></table></div><aside class=\"callout callout-try-this-now\"><p class=\"callout-label\">TRY THIS NOW</p><p>Stand up. Without moving your arms at all, shrug your shoulders up to your ears. That is elevation. Now pull them down hard, away from your ears. That is depression. Now reach your arms forward and let your shoulder blades slide apart around your ribs. That is protraction. Now pull them together behind you like you are pinching a pencil between them. That is retraction. Four movements, ten seconds, no equipment. <strong>Most of your future clients will not be able to do these on command the first time you ask.</strong> That inability is exactly what you are going to fix.</p></aside>",
"1.7.3": "<p>When a trainee hangs from a bar with the shoulders shrugged up to the ears, here is what happens:</p><ol><li><strong>The lats switch off.</strong> From an elevated position, the lat is mechanically compromised and cannot generate meaningful force. The trainee is now trying to do a pull-up with their arms and upper traps.</li><li><strong>Load transfers to the upper traps and neck.</strong> Tissue not built for that job takes the strain. Hence the trainee who gets neck tension after pull-up sessions.</li><li><strong>The space in the joint narrows.</strong> The gap between the arm bone and the bony roof above it shrinks, compressing the tendons and bursa that pass through it.</li><li><strong>Over months, this becomes shoulder impingement</strong> — pain at the front or top of the shoulder, worst overhead, that lingers and recurs.</li></ol><p>The correction is <em>not</em> the instruction &quot;don&#x27;t shrug.&quot; That is a description of the problem, not a solution. The correction is <strong>teaching active depression as the default loaded position</strong>, until it becomes automatic.</p><p class=\"rule\"><strong>A trainee who cannot depress their scapula under load is not ready for pull-ups — regardless of</strong> <strong>how strong their arms are.</strong></p><p>This is a hard rule. Uphold it and you will prevent more injuries in your career than any other single decision.</p>",
"1.7.4": "<p>These belong in <strong>every warm-up, every session, for the entirety of Stage 1.</strong> Four minutes.</p><h4><strong>Drill 1 — Scapular pull-up (active hang)</strong></h4><p>Hang from a bar with straight arms and relaxed shoulders. Without bending the elbows <em>at all</em>, pull the shoulder blades down and back so that your chest rises an inch or two and your whole body lifts slightly. Hold two seconds. Release slowly back to the passive hang.</p><p><strong>3 × 8.</strong></p><p><em>This is the most valuable single drill in Stage 1.</em> It teaches the exact starting position of a pull-up, in isolation, with no other demand attached. A trainee who owns eight clean scapular pull-ups has a completely different pull-up ahead of them than one who does not.</p><h4><strong>Drill 2 — Scapular pushup</strong></h4><p>Top of a pushup position, arms locked straight. Without bending the elbows, let your chest sink down between your shoulder blades (retraction), then push your upper back up toward the ceiling (protraction). Small movement, big effect.</p><p><strong>3 × 10.</strong></p><p>Teaches protraction and directly targets the serratus anterior — the muscle that makes overhead work safe.</p><h4><strong>Drill 3 — Support hold on parallel bars</strong></h4><p>Lock the arms straight, actively press the shoulders down away from the ears, ribs down, legs together, glutes lightly on.</p><p><strong>3 × 20–30 seconds.</strong></p><p>Teaches depression under compressive load. This is the prerequisite for dips and it is where the shoulder learns to stay stable when squeezed.</p><h4><strong>Drill 4 — Wall slide</strong></h4><p>Stand with your back to a wall, feet slightly forward, lower back flat against the wall. Place your forearms on the wall with elbows at shoulder height. Slide your arms overhead while keeping forearms in contact with the wall and without letting the lower back arch away from it.</p><p><strong>3 × 10.</strong></p><p>Trains overhead range with the trunk controlled, which is the whole point — overhead range that comes from arching the back is not overhead range.</p><h4><strong>Drill 5 — Band pull-apart or retraction hold</strong></h4><p>Arms straight in front, band in both hands. Pull apart using the shoulder blades, hold two seconds at full retraction, release slowly.</p><p><strong>3 × 12.</strong></p><p>Builds mid-back endurance against the rounded-shoulder posture that almost every new client brings through the door.</p>",
"1.7.5": "<p>One sentence that resolves most of the confusion in this area:</p><aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p><strong>Pushing movements finish in protraction. Pulling movements begin in retraction and depression.</strong></p></aside><p>At the top of a pushup, actively push the floor away and let the blades wrap forward around the ribs. At the start of a pull, set the blades down and back — <em>then</em> pull.</p><p>Get this backwards and you get predictable problems:</p><ul><li>A trainee who pushes with blades pinned back loses serratus engagement and never develops healthy overhead capacity.</li><li>A trainee who pulls with protracted blades loses lat engagement and dumps the load onto the biceps and the front of the shoulder joint.</li></ul><p>Both are common. Both resolve in two to four weeks with the drills above, which is one of the most satisfying corrections you will make as a coach because the client feels the difference immediately.</p>",
"1.7.6": "<p>Some clients simply do not have a usable overhead position on day one. Desk work, driving, phone use, and years of no overhead movement produce a stiff thoracic spine and short front-shoulder tissue.</p><p>Forcing overhead work on this body is how you create injuries. Building the position first takes six to eight weeks and is entirely worth it.</p><p><strong>The protocol:</strong></p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Element</strong></th><th><strong>Detail</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Wall slides</td><td>3 × 10, every session</td></tr><tr><td>Thoracic extension over a foam roller or rolled towel</td><td>2 × 30 sec</td></tr><tr><td>Thoracic rotations on all fours</td><td>2 × 8 each side</td></tr><tr><td>Lat stretch, hanging or overhead reach</td><td>2 × 30 sec</td></tr><tr><td>Doorway chest stretch</td><td>2 × 30 sec each side</td></tr><tr><td>Dead hangs</td><td>3 × 30 sec</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><strong>Meanwhile, in the actual program:</strong> use dips and bench dips instead of pike pushups. Vertical pushing still happens, just below shoulder height where the restriction does not bite. Re-test the overhead screen every three weeks.</p>",
"1.7.7": "<p>You are a trainer, not a clinician. Knowing the boundary is part of being a professional, not a limitation on being one.</p><p><strong>Stop the movement and refer to a physiotherapist or physician if you see or hear:</strong></p><ul><li>Sharp or pinching pain, as distinct from muscular effort or fatigue</li><li>Pain that persists at rest, or wakes the person at night</li><li>Numbness, tingling, or weakness travelling down the arm</li><li>Clicking or catching <em>accompanied by pain</em> (painless clicking is usually nothing)</li><li>Any sudden loss of range or strength</li><li>A feeling of the joint &quot;slipping&quot; or being unstable</li><li>Pain that has been present for more than two weeks without improving</li></ul><p><strong>What you do:</strong> remove the loaded position, keep training everything that does not hurt, and refer. Do not diagnose. Do not name a condition. Do not &quot;train through it.&quot; <strong>What you do not do:</strong> decide it is probably just a tight muscle, prescribe a stretch, and carry on. That is the decision that turns a two-week problem into a six-month one, and it is also the decision that ends careers when it goes badly.</p><aside class=\"callout callout-from-the-floor\"><p class=\"callout-label\">FROM THE FLOOR</p><p>A client mentions, casually, that their shoulder has been &quot;clicking and a bit sore&quot; for about three weeks, but it is fine, and can you just keep going.</p><p>The word that matters is &quot;three weeks.&quot; Three weeks of pain that has not resolved is not a tight muscle. Your response: stop loading that position today, keep training everything else, and refer out. Say it warmly and without alarm — &quot;Let&#x27;s get someone who specialises in this to look at it, and in the meantime we&#x27;ll train around it, so you don&#x27;t lose momentum.&quot;</p><p>Clients respect this. It reads as competence, not as a limitation. The trainer who says &quot;yeah, push through&quot; reads as confident for about a month and then reads as the reason they got hurt.</p></aside>",
"1.7-quiz": "<p>Five questions, ordered from straightforward to challenging.</p><ol><li>The four scapular movements are:</li></ol><ul class=\"options\"><li>A) Flexion, extension, abduction, adduction</li><li>B) Elevation, depression, protraction, retraction</li><li>C) Rotation, translation, gliding, tilting</li><li>D) Pronation, supination, inversion, eversion</li></ul><ol start=\"2\"><li>Which position is correct at the <em>start of a pull</em>?</li></ol><ul class=\"options\"><li>A) Protraction and elevation</li><li>B) Retraction and depression</li><li>C) Elevation only</li><li>D) Protraction only</li></ul><ol start=\"3\"><li>Hanging with shoulders shrugged to the ears reduces lat engagement because:</li></ol><ul class=\"options\"><li>A) The lats are anatomically detached in that position</li><li>B) From an elevated scapular position the lat is mechanically compromised and cannot generate meaningful force</li><li>C) The grip fails before the lats activate</li><li>D) Blood flow to the lats is restricted</li></ul><ol start=\"4\"><li>A trainee who cannot depress their scapula under load:</li></ol><ul class=\"options\"><li>A) Should train pull-ups anyway, since strength will develop the control</li><li>B) Is not ready for pull-ups, regardless of arm strength</li><li>C) Should use a wider grip to compensate</li><li>D) Should add weight to increase engagement</li></ul><ol start=\"5\"><li>A client reports front-of-shoulder pain during dips. Your first three actions are:</li></ol><ul class=\"options\"><li>A) Add weight to strengthen the position, cue harder, continue</li><li>B) Reduce depth immediately, check scapular control, reduce volume</li><li>C) Stretch the pectorals, apply ice, continue as normal</li><li>D) Switch to bench dips and say nothing further</li></ul>",
"1.8.1": "<p>Hanging from a bar looks like nothing. It is a prerequisite for everything.</p><p>Every bar skill in this sport begins from a hang. The pull-up, the muscle-up, the front lever, the human flag, every freestyle movement — all start with a body suspended from the hands. A trainee who is not comfortable hanging is not going to be comfortable doing anything else up there.</p><p>Hanging also does something no other exercise does: it <strong>decompresses</strong> the spine and loads the shoulder in traction rather than compression. For a client who sits eight hours a day, thirty seconds of hanging can feel better than a massage.</p>",
"1.8.2": "<p>Two completely different positions with the same name in common usage. Know the difference cold.</p><p><strong>Passive (dead) hang.</strong> Shoulders relaxed, blades elevated, body hanging from the joint structure rather than held by muscle. Grip and connective tissue are working; the shoulder muscles are relatively quiet.</p><p><em>Use for:</em> grip endurance, spinal decompression, shoulder mobility and traction, general tolerance of hanging. Perfectly safe for a healthy shoulder and genuinely therapeutic for most desk workers.</p><p><strong>Active hang.</strong> Shoulder blades actively depressed and slightly retracted, chest lifted, body held by muscle. Lats, lower traps, and serratus engaged. The body feels shorter and tighter.</p><p><em>Use for:</em> the starting position of every pull, scapular strength, the prerequisite position for pull-ups and levers.</p><aside class=\"callout callout-coach-s-eye\"><p class=\"callout-label\">COACH&#x27;S EYE</p><p>A very common error, including among trainers who should know better, is treating passive hangs as always wrong and active hangs as always right. Both are correct — for different jobs.</p><p>The actual rule: <strong>passive hangs for mobility and grip work; active hangs whenever you are about to pull.</strong> A trainee should be able to move smoothly between the two on command. That transition <em>is</em> the scapular pull-up from Module 1.7.</p></aside>",
"1.8.3": "<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Step</strong></th><th><strong>Movement</strong></th><th><strong>Target before advancing</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Supported hang, feet partly on the ground</td><td>3 × 30 sec</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Passive hang, two hands</td><td>3 × 30 sec</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Passive hang</td><td>1 × 60 sec</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Active hang</td><td>3 × 20 sec</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Scapular pull-ups</td><td>3 × 10</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Active hang, one leg lifted</td><td>3 × 20 sec</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Single-arm assisted hang (one hand on wrist)</td><td>3 × 15 sec each</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Single-arm hang</td><td>3 × 10 sec each</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><strong>Targets worth aiming for by the end of Stage 1:</strong> 60 seconds passive hang, 30 seconds active hang, 10 clean scapular pull-ups.</p>",
"1.8.4": "<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Grip</strong></th><th><strong>Description</strong></th><th><strong>Notes</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Pronated</td><td>Palms facing away</td><td>Standard. Hardest on grip.</td></tr><tr><td>Supinated</td><td>Palms facing you</td><td>Easier pulling, more biceps</td></tr><tr><td>Neutral</td><td>Palms facing each other</td><td>Most shoulder-friendly</td></tr><tr><td>False grip</td><td>Thumb on top of the bar with the hand</td><td>For muscle-ups later. Introduce in Stage 2.</td></tr><tr><td>Thumbless</td><td>Thumb alongside fingers</td><td>Some prefer it for lat engagement. Less secure — never use it high off the ground.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><strong>Bar thickness matters more than people expect.</strong> A thicker bar dramatically increases grip demand. If a client&#x27;s grip is failing on a thick park bar, it is not necessarily a grip weakness — it may just be an inappropriate bar for their current level. Find a thinner one, or use a towel or strap to reduce the diameter.</p>",
"1.8.5": "<p>For anyone who sits all day, this two-minute daily protocol is the highest return-on-time intervention in Stage 1:</p><ol><li>Passive hang — 30 seconds</li><li>Rest 30 seconds</li><li>Active hang — 20 seconds</li><li>Rest 30 seconds</li><li>Scapular pull-ups — 8 reps</li></ol><p>Every day, including rest days. It builds grip, it builds scapular control, it decompresses the spine, it opens the shoulders, and it costs two minutes.</p><aside class=\"callout callout-try-this-now\"><p class=\"callout-label\">TRY THIS NOW</p><p>Go and hang from something for thirty seconds. Right now. Notice: where does it burn first? For most people it is the hands and forearms, not the shoulders or back. <strong>That is information.</strong> It means grip is currently your limiter, and everything you do on a bar is going to be capped by it until you address it — which is the next module.</p></aside>",
"1.8-quiz": "<p>Five questions, ordered from straightforward to challenging.</p><ol><li>In a passive (dead) hang, the shoulder blades are:</li></ol><ul class=\"options\"><li>A) Depressed and retracted</li><li>B) Elevated, with the body hanging from joint structure</li><li>C) Protracted and elevated</li><li>D) Actively rotating</li></ul><ol start=\"2\"><li>The Stage 1 hanging targets are:</li></ol><ul class=\"options\"><li>A) 30-second passive, 15-second active, 5 scapular pull-ups</li><li>B) 60-second passive, 30-second active, 10 scapular pull-ups</li><li>C) 90-second passive, 60-second active, 20 scapular pull-ups</li><li>D) 45-second passive, 45-second active, 15 scapular pull-ups</li></ul><ol start=\"3\"><li>Hanging is particularly valuable for a desk-working client because it:</li></ol><ul class=\"options\"><li>A) Builds maximal pulling strength quickly</li><li>B) Decompresses the spine and loads the shoulder in traction rather than compression</li><li>C) Replaces the need for pulling exercises</li><li>D) Improves cardiovascular fitness</li></ul><ol start=\"4\"><li>Which statement about the two hang types is correct?</li></ol><ul class=\"options\"><li>A) Passive hangs are always incorrect and should be avoided</li><li>B) Active hangs are unsafe for beginners</li><li>C) Both are correct for different jobs — passive for mobility and grip, active before pulling</li><li>D) The two are interchangeable</li></ul><ol start=\"5\"><li>A client&#x27;s grip fails after 12 seconds on a thick park bar. The two explanations to consider are:</li></ol><ul class=\"options\"><li>A) Poor scapular control, or insufficient warm-up</li><li>B) Genuine grip weakness, or an inappropriately thick bar for their current level</li><li>C) Weak lats, or a mobility restriction</li><li>D) Low body weight, or poor footwear</li></ul>",
"1.9.1": "<p>Here is a scenario you will meet constantly. A client&#x27;s lats are strong enough for eight pull-ups. Their hands let go at five.</p><p>They report this as &quot;my back gave out.&quot; It did not. Their grip did, and because grip failure and back failure feel similar from the inside — the set ends, the body comes off the bar — they misattribute it.</p><p>In calisthenics, <strong>grip is the connection between you and everything.</strong> Every bar movement is transmitted through your hands. If the hands are the weak link, every other capacity you have built is capped behind them.</p><p>And grip is trainable, quickly. It responds well and it responds fast. It is one of the most satisfying things to coach because the results show up in weeks.</p>",
"1.9.2": "<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Type</strong></th><th><strong>What it does</strong></th><th><strong>Trained by</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Support grip</strong></td><td>Holding on while hanging — the most important for calisthenics</td><td>Hangs, farmer carries, bar holds</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Crush grip</strong></td><td>Squeezing something closed</td><td>Hand grippers, towel squeezes</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pinch grip</strong></td><td>Holding between thumb and fingers</td><td>Plate pinches, thick-bar work</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>For calisthenics, <strong>support grip dominates.</strong> Prioritise it. Crush and pinch grip are useful extras, not the main event.</p>",
"1.9.3": "<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Step</strong></th><th><strong>Movement</strong></th><th><strong>Target</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Passive hang</td><td>3 × 30 sec</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Passive hang</td><td>1 × 60 sec</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Towel-over-bar hang</td><td>3 × 20 sec</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Thick-bar or towel-grip hang</td><td>3 × 30 sec</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Single-arm assisted hang</td><td>3 × 15 sec each</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Single-arm hang</td><td>3 × 10 sec each</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Single-arm hang</td><td>3 × 20 sec each</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",
"1.9.4": "<p>Everything in calisthenics trains the <strong>flexors</strong> — the muscles that close the hand and curl the wrist. Hanging, gripping, pulling: all flexors.</p><p>Almost nothing trains the <strong>extensors</strong> — the muscles on the back of the forearm that open the hand and extend the wrist.</p><p>Train one side of a joint for two years and neglect the other, and you get the classic overuse pattern: elbow tendinopathy, wrist pain, forearm tightness that never seems to resolve no matter how much you stretch it.</p><p><strong>The balance protocol, twice a week, five minutes:</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Finger extensions.</strong> Fingertips on the floor, press down and lift the palm. 3 × 15. Or use a rubber band around the fingers and open the hand against it.</li><li><strong>Reverse wrist curls.</strong> Palm facing down, curl the wrist upward against light resistance or just gravity. 3 × 15.</li><li><strong>Wrist extension holds.</strong> Palms flat on the floor, fingers forward, weight over the hands. 3 × 20 sec.</li><li><strong>Rice bucket or towel work.</strong> Open and close the hand in resistance; wring out a wet towel. 2 minutes.</li><li><strong>Forearm stretches.</strong> Flexors and extensors, 30 seconds each.</li></ol><aside class=\"callout callout-coach-s-eye\"><p class=\"callout-label\">COACH&#x27;S EYE</p><p>When a calisthenics trainee gets elbow pain — typically on the outside or inside of the elbow — the cause is almost never the elbow. It is the accumulated forearm imbalance plus a sudden jump in hanging or straight-arm volume. Address the extensors, reduce volume for two weeks, and check what they added to their training in the month before it started. There is nearly always a spike.</p></aside>",
"1.9.5": "<p>Unglamorous, and the reason plenty of people miss training sessions.</p><p><strong>Chalk</strong> dries the hands and improves friction, and genuinely matters in humid conditions — relevant across most of India for much of the year. Liquid chalk is more practical than block chalk and less likely to get you banned from a facility.</p><p><strong>Calluses</strong> are useful up to a point. Thick raised calluses, however, are what tear off during a hard set — a rip that costs a week of training. Manage them: file them down with a pumice stone or file every week or two, keep them flat rather than raised, and moisturise at night.</p><p><strong>Tears</strong> happen. Keep the area clean, cover it, let it heal, and train leg and core work in the meantime. Do not grip on an open tear; you will make it larger and risk infection.</p>",
"1.9-quiz": "<p>Five questions, ordered from straightforward to challenging.</p><ol><li>The three types of grip are:</li></ol><ul class=\"options\"><li>A) Static, dynamic, isometric</li><li>B) Support, crush, pinch</li><li>C) Open, closed, hook</li><li>D) Pronated, supinated, neutral</li></ul><ol start=\"2\"><li>Which statement about grip training is most accurate?</li></ol><ul class=\"options\"><li>A) Grip is largely genetic and responds slowly to training</li><li>B) Grip responds well and relatively quickly to training, with results in weeks</li><li>C) Grip should only be trained after two years of general training</li><li>D) Grip work should be avoided as it interferes with pulling strength</li></ul><ol start=\"3\"><li>Calisthenics trainees develop forearm imbalances because:</li></ol><ul class=\"options\"><li>A) Hanging and pulling train the flexors while almost nothing trains the extensors</li><li>B) The extensors are naturally stronger and overpower the flexors</li><li>C) Grip work damages the forearm muscles</li><li>D) The forearms are not used in most calisthenics movements</li></ul><ol start=\"4\"><li>A client says their &quot;back gave out&quot; at five pull-ups when their lats are strong enough for eight. The likely explanation is:</li></ol><ul class=\"options\"><li>A) Poor scapular control</li><li>B) Grip failure, misattributed because both feel similar from the inside</li><li>C) Inadequate warm-up</li><li>D) A mobility restriction</li></ul><ol start=\"5\"><li>A client presents with outer-elbow pain after adding a lot of hanging work. Your first two questions concern:</li></ol><ul class=\"options\"><li>A) Their sleep and their diet</li><li>B) Their grip width and their bar type</li><li>C) What they added to their training in the previous month, and their forearm extensor work</li><li>D) Their shoe choice and warm-up length</li></ul>",
"1.10.1": "<p>Most beginners do one of two things under load: they hold their breath, or they breathe randomly. Both are problems, and both come from the same confusion — thinking that bracing and breath-holding are the same thing.</p><p>They are not. <strong>Bracing is creating trunk stiffness. Breath-holding is one way to do that, and it is not usually</strong> <strong>the right one.</strong></p>",
"1.10.2": "<p>Bracing means creating tension through the abdominal wall so that force can transfer through the trunk without the spine moving.</p><p>Imagine an aluminium can. Empty and open, you can crush it with one hand. Sealed and pressurised, it supports considerable weight. Your trunk works the same way — pressure inside the abdominal cavity, with the muscular wall around it under tension, produces a rigid cylinder.</p><p><strong>How to brace, properly:</strong></p><ol><li>Breathe in through the nose, down into the belly and lower ribs — not up into the chest and shoulders.</li><li>Tense the abdominal wall <strong>all the way around</strong>, as though preparing for a light punch to the stomach. Not sucking in. Not pushing out. Tensing outward against a 360-degree circumference.</li><li>Keep the ribs down. Do not let the ribcage flare upward — that position disconnects the abs from doing their job.</li><li>Maintain that tension while breathing shallowly through the movement.</li></ol><aside class=\"callout callout-try-this-now\"><p class=\"callout-label\">TRY THIS NOW</p><p>Put your hands on the sides of your lower ribs. Breathe in and try to push your hands apart sideways, without your shoulders rising. That is diaphragmatic breathing. Most adults breathe almost entirely with the chest and upper shoulders, which produces less air, more neck tension, and a worse brace. Practise this for one minute and you will feel your shoulders drop.</p></aside>",
"1.10.3": "<p><strong>The Valsalva manoeuvre</strong> — a deep breath held against a closed throat — maximises trunk stiffness and is legitimate for maximal-effort single reps. It also raises blood pressure sharply and should not be a beginner&#x27;s default.</p><p><strong>Stage 1 rules:</strong></p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Situation</strong></th><th><strong>Approach</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Moderate reps (8–15)</td><td>Inhale on the eccentric, exhale on the concentric</td></tr><tr><td>Isometric holds (plank, hollow, L-sit)</td><td>Brace, then breathe shallowly and continuously — <strong>never hold</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Long hangs</td><td>Relaxed, normal breathing</td></tr><tr><td>Maximal single effort</td><td>Brief breath-hold acceptable for the rep itself</td></tr><tr><td>Any client with blood pressure concerns</td><td>Never hold. Breathe throughout. Flag for medical clearance.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><strong>The most common beginner error is holding the breath through a plank or hollow hold.</strong> They go red, shake, and quit at 20 seconds — not because their core failed but because they ran out of air. Cue them to speak a sentence out loud mid-hold; if they cannot, they are holding their breath.</p><aside class=\"callout callout-coach-s-eye\"><p class=\"callout-label\">COACH&#x27;S EYE</p><p>&quot;Can you talk to me?&quot; is one of the best diagnostic questions in coaching. Mid-plank, mid-hang, mid-hold — if the client cannot answer you, they are breath-holding. It is a fault that looks like weakness and disappears within one session once named.</p></aside>",
"1.10.4": "<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Movement</strong></th><th><strong>Inhale</strong></th><th><strong>Exhale</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Pushup</td><td>Lowering</td><td>Pressing up</td></tr><tr><td>Pull-up</td><td>Lowering</td><td>Pulling up</td></tr><tr><td>Dip</td><td>Lowering</td><td>Pressing up</td></tr><tr><td>Squat</td><td>Descending</td><td>Standing up</td></tr><tr><td>Hip bridge</td><td>Lowering</td><td>Driving up</td></tr><tr><td>Plank / hollow / L-sit</td><td>Continuous shallow breathing</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>Hang</td><td>Relaxed continuous</td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The general principle: <strong>exhale on the effort.</strong> Inhale during the eccentric, when the muscle is lengthening under control.</p>",
"1.10.5": "<p>Between sets and after training, breathing is an active recovery lever that costs nothing.</p><p><strong>Between sets:</strong> 3–4 slow nasal breaths, exhale longer than inhale. Drops the heart rate faster than passive waiting and gets you to the next set in better shape.</p><p><strong>After training:</strong> 2–3 minutes of 4-second inhale, 8-second exhale. Shifts the nervous system from sympathetic to parasympathetic — from training mode into recovery mode, where the actual adaptation happens.</p><p><strong>Before a hard attempt:</strong> one deep breath, one full exhale, then brace. Most beginners approach a maximal attempt already tense and shallow-breathing, which reduces force output.</p>",
"1.10-quiz": "<p>Five questions, ordered from straightforward to challenging.</p><ol><li>The difference between bracing and breath-holding is that:</li></ol><ul class=\"options\"><li>A) They are the same thing under different names</li><li>B) Bracing creates trunk stiffness; breath-holding is one way to do that, and usually not the right one</li><li>C) Bracing applies only to the lower back</li><li>D) Breath-holding is safer for beginners</li></ul><ol start=\"2\"><li>The general breathing rule for repetition work is:</li></ol><ul class=\"options\"><li>A) Inhale on the effort, exhale on the lowering</li><li>B) Exhale on the effort, inhale during the eccentric</li><li>C) Hold throughout the set</li><li>D) Breathe only between reps</li></ul><ol start=\"3\"><li>Correct bracing involves:</li></ol><ul class=\"options\"><li>A) Sucking the stomach inward as hard as possible</li><li>B) Pushing the abdomen outward while flaring the ribs</li><li>C) Breathing into the belly and lower ribs, then tensing the abdominal wall 360 degrees with ribs down</li><li>D) Holding maximum air in the chest</li></ul><ol start=\"4\"><li>During a plank or hollow hold, the trainee should:</li></ol><ul class=\"options\"><li>A) Hold the breath for maximum stability</li><li>B) Brace, then breathe shallowly and continuously</li><li>C) Breathe deeply into the chest</li><li>D) Exhale fully and hold empty</li></ul><ol start=\"5\"><li>Which single question reveals whether a client is holding their breath during a hold?</li></ol><ul class=\"options\"><li>A) &quot;Does it hurt?&quot;</li><li>B) &quot;How many seconds left?&quot;</li><li>C) &quot;Can you talk to me?&quot;</li><li>D) &quot;Are you bracing?&quot;</li></ul>",
"1.11.1": "<p>Most people think a program is a list of exercises. It is not. A list of exercises is a workout. A <strong>program</strong> is a plan for getting progressively stronger over time without breaking down.</p><p>The difference is that a program has a rule for what changes next week.</p><p>Six decisions make one. Make them in order.</p>",
"1.11.2": "<p>Everything downstream depends on this, and for Stage 1 the honest answer is almost always the same: <strong>general</strong> <strong>strength and movement competency.</strong></p><p>Beginners do not need specialisation; they need a base. When a new client asks to train exclusively for the front lever, the correct answer is that the fastest route to the front lever is six months of not training the front lever.</p><p>Say it with the reasoning attached: &quot;You&#x27;ll get there faster if we build the foundation first. Right now your shoulders and elbows can&#x27;t handle the loading that skill requires. Give me three months and you&#x27;ll progress through the lever work in half the time it would otherwise take.&quot;</p>",
"1.11.3": "<p>Cover every pattern. One exercise per pattern. Use the template:</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Pattern</strong></th><th><strong>Pick one at your current ladder step</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Vertical pull</td><td>Active hang / band pull-up / negative / full pull-up</td></tr><tr><td>Horizontal push</td><td>Incline / knee / full pushup</td></tr><tr><td>Horizontal pull</td><td>Inverted row at appropriate bar height</td></tr><tr><td>Vertical push</td><td>Bench dip / assisted dip / full dip / pike pushup</td></tr><tr><td>Squat</td><td>Box squat / bodyweight squat / split squat</td></tr><tr><td>Hinge</td><td>Hip bridge / single-leg bridge / Nordic negative</td></tr><tr><td>Core</td><td>Plank / hollow hold / hanging knee raise</td></tr><tr><td>Anti-rotation or lateral</td><td>Side plank / bird dog / dead bug</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Eight movements. That is a complete beginner program, and it is enough. Resist the urge to add more — the most common beginner program error is not too little variety, it is too much.</p><aside class=\"callout callout-coach-s-eye\"><p class=\"callout-label\">COACH&#x27;S EYE</p><p>A program with sixteen exercises in it is usually a program written by someone who could not decide. It looks thorough and trains nothing hard enough to force adaptation. Eight movements, done seriously, three times a week, will outperform it every time.</p></aside>",
"1.11.4": "<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Goal</strong></th><th><strong>Sets</strong></th><th><strong>Reps</strong></th><th><strong>Rest</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Maximal strength</td><td>3–5</td><td>3–6</td><td>2–3 min</td></tr><tr><td>Hypertrophy (size)</td><td>3–4</td><td>8–12</td><td>60–90 sec</td></tr><tr><td>Endurance</td><td>2–3</td><td>15+</td><td>30–60 sec</td></tr><tr><td>Static holds</td><td>3–4</td><td>20–45 sec</td><td>60–90 sec</td></tr><tr><td>Skill practice</td><td>5–8</td><td>1–3</td><td>as needed, never to fatigue</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><strong>Stage 1 default: 3 sets of 8–12 reps, 90 seconds rest.</strong></p><p>Why this range: it builds both strength and size, it is forgiving of imperfect technique, and — most importantly — it gives a beginner enough repetitions for the nervous system to learn the pattern. Three sets of three would build strength but provide far too little practice.</p><p>Leave two to three reps in reserve on most sets, one to two on the final set of a key movement, and stop the moment form degrades regardless of the number on the page.</p>",
"1.11.5": "<h4><strong>Option A — Full body, 3 days/week. Recommended for Stage 1.</strong></h4><p>Monday, Wednesday, Friday. Every session hits every pattern.</p><p><em>Why this wins for beginners:</em> highest frequency per movement pattern. Since early progress is largely skill acquisition (Module 1.2), practising each pattern three times a week beats training it once. Also the most forgiving structure — miss a session and you have still trained everything twice.</p><h4><strong>Option B — Upper / Lower split, 4 days/week.</strong></h4><p>Upper Monday and Thursday, Lower Tuesday and Friday. For trainees who want more volume per session and have the recovery capacity for it.</p><h4><strong>Option C — Push / Pull split, 4 days/week.</strong></h4><p>Legitimate, but risky for beginners: if the pull day is the one that gets skipped when life gets busy, you have recreated the exact imbalance the whole course warns about. Use later, with clients whose consistency you trust.</p><h4><strong>Option D — Two days/week.</strong></h4><p>Not a compromise to apologise for. Two well-designed full-body sessions a week produce real progress, and for a client with genuine time constraints, two sessions they actually complete beats four they abandon in week three. <strong>Program for</strong> <strong>the life the client has, not the life they wish they had.</strong></p>",
"1.11.6": "<p>Order matters. Hardest and most skill-dependent first, while the nervous system is fresh.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Order</strong></th><th><strong>Slot</strong></th><th><strong>Reason</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Warm-up (12 min)</td><td>Non-negotiable</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Vertical pull</td><td>Highest skill demand, needs to be fresh</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Horizontal push</td><td>Large movement, pairs with pull</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Horizontal pull</td><td>Balance the push</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Vertical push</td><td>Second pressing pattern</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Squat</td><td>Lower body, fatiguing</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Hinge</td><td>Posterior chain</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Core</td><td>Limiter — do not fatigue it early</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Anti-rotation / lateral</td><td>Round out the planes</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Cool-down (6–8 min)</td><td>Recovery</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Session length: roughly 55–70 minutes.</p><h4><strong>Sample session — Full body, intermediate beginner</strong></h4><p><strong>Warm-up:</strong> full Stage 1 warm-up (Module 1.5), 12 minutes</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Exercise</strong></th><th><strong>Sets × Reps</strong></th><th><strong>Tempo</strong></th><th><strong>Rest</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Band-assisted pull-up</td><td>4 × 6</td><td>2-0-1-0</td><td>2 min</td></tr><tr><td>Full pushup</td><td>3 × 10</td><td>2-1-1-0</td><td>90 sec</td></tr><tr><td>Inverted row, feet elevated</td><td>3 × 10</td><td>2-1-1-1</td><td>90 sec</td></tr><tr><td>Parallel bar dip, assisted</td><td>3 × 8</td><td>2-0-1-0</td><td>90 sec</td></tr><tr><td>Bodyweight squat</td><td>3 × 15</td><td>2-0-1-0</td><td>90 sec</td></tr><tr><td>Single-leg hip bridge</td><td>3 × 12 each</td><td>2-1-1-1</td><td>60 sec</td></tr><tr><td>Hollow body hold</td><td>3 × 25 sec</td><td>—</td><td>60 sec</td></tr><tr><td>Side plank</td><td>3 × 30 sec each</td><td>—</td><td>45 sec</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><strong>Cool-down:</strong> Module 1.5 protocol, 7 minutes</p>",
"1.11.7": "<p>A program without an explicit progression rule is a workout you repeat until it stops working. <strong>Write the rule down</strong> <strong>before week one.</strong></p><h4><strong>The Stage 1 double progression</strong></h4><p>This is the method to default to. It handles the awkward gap between ladder steps.</p><ol><li>Start at <strong>3 × 8</strong> on a given ladder step.</li><li>Each week, add one rep to one set: 8/8/8 → 9/8/8 → 9/9/8 → 9/9/9 → 10/9/9...</li><li>When you reach <strong>3 × 12 with clean form</strong>, move up one ladder step.</li><li>Restart the new step at 3 × 8.</li></ol><p>It looks slow on paper. Run the arithmetic: that is roughly one ladder step every eight to twelve weeks, per pattern. Over a year, that is four or five steps — which takes someone from knee pushups to archer pushups, and from a dead hang to real pull-ups.</p><h4><strong>When double progression stalls</strong></h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Situation</strong></th><th><strong>Response</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Stuck at the same reps for 3 weeks</td><td>Add a set, or slow the tempo, before changing the exercise</td></tr><tr><td>Stuck and fatigue is high</td><td>Deload a week, then retry</td></tr><tr><td>Reps rising but form degrading</td><td>Stop progressing reps; fix form at current volume</td></tr><tr><td>Jump between ladder steps is too big</td><td>Use tempo as a bridge: full pushups at 4-second eccentric before full pushups at normal tempo</td></tr></tbody></table></div><aside class=\"callout callout-coach-s-eye\"><p class=\"callout-label\">COACH&#x27;S EYE</p><p>The gap between &quot;assisted pull-up&quot; and &quot;full pull-up&quot; is where most programs break. The fix is not willpower — it is intermediate steps. Reduce the band thickness. Add a tempo requirement. Use negatives with longer lowers. Use a mix in the same set: two full reps, then three assisted. <strong>Your job as a coach is to manufacture a step where the ladder has none.</strong></p></aside>",
"1.11.8": "<p>Every fourth to sixth week, cut volume roughly in half for one week. Same exercises, same difficulty, fewer sets. Three sets becomes two; four becomes two.</p><p>Beginners frequently feel they do not need this. They mostly do not — until suddenly they do, usually presenting as a stubborn plateau, poor sleep, and a persistent small ache. Building the deload into the plan from the start is both better training and a professional standard worth modelling for clients.</p><h4><strong>Twelve-week structure</strong></h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Weeks</strong></th><th><strong>Focus</strong></th><th><strong>Notes</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1–2</td><td>Learn the patterns</td><td>Low volume, high attention to form. Do not chase numbers.</td></tr><tr><td>3–5</td><td>Build</td><td>Double progression running on every pattern</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Deload</td><td>Half volume</td></tr><tr><td>7–10</td><td>Build</td><td>Continue progression; expect 1–2 ladder-step advances</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Peak</td><td>Highest volume of the block</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Deload and retest</td><td>Half volume, then re-run baseline tests</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Then start a new block from the new baseline. <strong>This is what training looks like forever.</strong> Not endless linear increase, but blocks of building punctuated by recovery, with retesting to prove it worked.</p>",
"1.11.9": "<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Date</strong></th><th><strong>Exercise</strong></th><th><strong>Ladder step</strong></th><th><strong>Sets × Reps</strong></th><th><strong>RIR</strong></th><th><strong>Notes</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>Form quality, energy, sleep, any pain</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The <strong>notes</strong> column is the one everybody skips and the one that matters most. &quot;Left shoulder tight on dips&quot; appearing three sessions in a row is actionable information <em>before</em> it becomes an injury. Sleep noted as four hours on a session where everything felt heavy explains a bad day that would otherwise look like a plateau.</p><aside class=\"callout callout-from-the-floor\"><p class=\"callout-label\">FROM THE FLOOR</p><p>A client comes in at week ten convinced they have made no progress and considering quitting. This happens constantly, and it is one of the moments where you either keep a client for years or lose them.</p><p>Open the log. Week one: incline pushups on a bench, 3 × 8; dead hang 15 seconds; box squats. Week ten: full pushups 3 × 11; band pull-ups 3 × 6; full-depth squats 3 × 18.</p><p>They genuinely did not notice, because progress is invisible from the inside — each week feels roughly as hard as the last, which is precisely what correct programming feels like. <strong>The log is not administration. It is the tool that keeps clients training.</strong></p></aside>",
"1.11-practical": "<ol><li>Using the Stage 1 template, write a complete 3-day full-body week for yourself. Include the full warm-up, every exercise with sets, reps, tempo, and rest, and the cool-down. State your ladder step for every pattern and your explicit progression rule.</li><li>Write a second program for this client: 45 years old, office job, no training history, cannot perform a single pull-up or full pushup, wrists tight, overhead screen failed, 3 hours per week available. Justify every regression you chose.</li><li>Write a third program for this client: 22 years old, plays football, trains 5 days a week already, can do 12 pull-ups and 30 pushups, wants a muscle-up, has never trained legs or hinge patterns. Identify their actual priority — which may not be what they asked for.</li><li>Map out a 12-week block for client two, including deload weeks and retest points.</li><li><strong>Trainer track:</strong> write, in plain beginner language, the explanation you would give for why their program includes a pulling movement for every pushing movement.</li></ol>",
"1.12.1": "<p>You already know from Module 1.2 that adaptation happens during recovery, not during the session. Most trainees accept that intellectually and then behave as though training is the only variable they control.</p><p>A client training three excellent sessions a week on five hours of sleep will progress worse than a client training two mediocre sessions on eight. That is not a motivational slogan; it is just how adaptation works.</p><p>Recovery is also where you have the most leverage as a coach, because most clients are doing it badly and do not know it.</p>",
"1.12.2": "<p>The single most important recovery variable. Nothing else is close.</p><p><strong>What sleep does for training specifically:</strong> growth hormone release concentrates in deep sleep; tissue repair happens overnight; motor learning — the skill acquisition that drives beginner progress — is consolidated during sleep; and appetite-regulating hormones normalise.</p><p><strong>What insufficient sleep does:</strong> reduces force output measurably, impairs motor learning so skills take longer to acquire, raises perceived exertion so training feels harder, increases injury risk, and reduces recovery between sessions.</p><p><strong>Practical targets:</strong> 7–9 hours for most adults. Consistent bed and wake times matter nearly as much as duration — the body runs on rhythm.</p><p><strong>Basic sleep hygiene worth telling clients:</strong></p><ul><li>Consistent sleep and wake times, including weekends</li><li>A dark, cool, quiet room</li><li>Reduce bright screens in the hour before bed</li><li>No caffeine after early afternoon — its effects last far longer than people assume</li><li>If training late in the evening leaves them wired, shift the session earlier or lengthen the breathing-based cool-down</li></ul><aside class=\"callout callout-coach-s-eye\"><p class=\"callout-label\">COACH&#x27;S EYE</p><p>When a client plateaus, ask about sleep before you touch the program. A trainer who fixes a plateau by finding out the client has been sleeping five hours for a month is doing better coaching than one who adds a fourth session. And the conversation itself signals that you are paying attention to them as a person, not just as a set of numbers.</p></aside>",
"1.12.3": "<p>Rest does not mean lying still.</p><p><strong>Complete rest</strong> — appropriate when genuinely fatigued, ill, or in a heavy block.</p><p><strong>Active recovery</strong> — light movement that promotes blood flow without adding fatigue: walking, easy cycling, swimming, mobility work, the two-minute hanging protocol. Usually better than complete rest for a beginner.</p><p><strong>Stage 1 default:</strong> three training days, two active recovery days, two flexible days. Beginners recover faster than they expect because their absolute loads are low — the limiting factor is more often connective tissue and skill consolidation than muscular fatigue.</p>",
"1.12.4": "<p>Teach clients to notice these, and ask about them yourself:</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Sign</strong></th><th><strong>What it suggests</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Resting heart rate elevated several days running</td><td>Systemic fatigue</td></tr><tr><td>Sleep worsening despite training hard</td><td>Nervous system overstimulated</td></tr><tr><td>Motivation dropping sharply</td><td>Often the first signal, and usually dismissed</td></tr><tr><td>Performance declining across multiple sessions</td><td>Fatigue exceeding recovery</td></tr><tr><td>Persistent small aches</td><td>Tissue not recovering between sessions</td></tr><tr><td>Getting ill more often</td><td>Accumulated stress load</td></tr><tr><td>Irritability, low mood</td><td>Under-recovery is systemic, not just muscular</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>One bad session means nothing. <strong>Three or four in a row, plus two or more of the signs above, means take a</strong> <strong>deload week.</strong> Not next month. This week.</p>",
"1.12.5": "<p>A clear boundary first, because it matters both professionally and legally:</p><aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p><strong>As a calisthenics trainer, you can give general nutrition guidance. You cannot prescribe diets, set calorie or</strong> <strong>macronutrient targets for individuals, or advise on nutrition for medical conditions.</strong> That is a registered dietitian&#x27;s or doctor&#x27;s work. Refer for anything specific, anything medical, and anything to do with disordered eating.</p></aside><p>Within that boundary, general principles you can absolutely discuss:</p><p><strong>Protein.</strong> Muscle repair requires protein. Most people training for strength benefit from including a protein source at each main meal — pulses, dairy, eggs, meat, fish, soy, whatever fits their diet and budget. This is general education, not a prescription.</p><p><strong>Total food intake.</strong> Training hard while eating far too little means training without the raw material to recover. Progress stalls, and it often looks like a programming problem when it is not.</p><p><strong>Hydration.</strong> Dehydration reduces performance and increases perceived effort. This matters more in hot and humid climates — relevant for much of India for much of the year. Encourage water before, during, and after training, and more on hot days.</p><p><strong>Timing.</strong> Far less important than total intake for a beginner. A useful general habit: something to eat within a couple of hours after training. Beyond that, do not over-complicate it.</p><p><strong>What to leave alone.</strong> Supplements beyond the obvious basics, anything dramatic, any restrictive protocol, anything you read on social media. A beginner who sleeps properly, eats enough real food, and trains consistently does not need a supplement stack, and selling them one is a good way to lose their trust.</p><aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p><strong>A note on how you talk about bodies.</strong> In bodyweight training, weight directly affects performance, which means the topic will come up. Handle it carefully. Never comment on a client&#x27;s body unprompted. Never imply that a client would progress faster if they were lighter, even when it is mechanically true. If a client raises weight loss, keep it to general principles and refer to an appropriate professional for anything specific.</p><p>If you ever notice signs of disordered eating — food rules that cause distress, secrecy around eating, exercising to compensate for eating, excessive concern with body shape — <strong>that is a referral, immediately and gently, and never something to coach</strong> <strong>through.</strong> This is a genuine duty of care, not a technicality.</p></aside>",
"1.12.6": "<p>Training stress is not the only stress the body is recovering from. Work pressure, family demands, financial worry, poor sleep, illness, travel, heat — all draw on the same recovery capacity.</p><p>A client under heavy life stress needs <strong>less</strong> training volume, not more, even though more is often what they want, because training is the one part of their life that feels controllable.</p><p>This is where a trainer earns their money. Reading the person, not just the program:</p><ul><li>Client had a brutal work week and slept four hours? Run the session at 70%. Focus on technique. Do not test anything.</li><li>Client is ill? No training. Fully. &quot;Train through a cold&quot; is how a cold becomes two weeks off.</li><li>Client is travelling? Give them a three-exercise floor-only program and tell them maintenance is a win.</li><li>Client is in a genuinely difficult period of life? Reduce to two sessions and protect consistency above all else. Consistency is the asset. Everything else is negotiable.</li></ul>",
"1.12-quiz": "<p>Five questions, ordered from straightforward to challenging.</p><ol><li>Sleep matters for beginner progress specifically because:</li></ol><ul class=\"options\"><li>A) It burns additional calories</li><li>B) Motor learning — the skill acquisition driving early gains — is consolidated during sleep</li><li>C) It increases muscle fibre count</li><li>D) It replaces the need for rest days</li></ul><ol start=\"2\"><li>Which is usually the <em>first</em> sign of under-recovery, and the most commonly dismissed?</li></ol><ul class=\"options\"><li>A) Elevated resting heart rate</li><li>B) A sharp drop in motivation</li><li>C) Persistent small aches</li><li>D) Getting ill more often</li></ul><ol start=\"3\"><li>As a calisthenics trainer, you may:</li></ol><ul class=\"options\"><li>A) Prescribe individual calorie and macronutrient targets</li><li>B) Give general nutrition education — protein sources, adequate intake, hydration, rough timing</li><li>C) Advise on nutrition for medical conditions</li><li>D) Recommend supplement protocols for specific outcomes</li></ul><ol start=\"4\"><li>Which would prompt an immediate, gentle referral regarding eating behaviour?</li></ol><ul class=\"options\"><li>A) A client asking what to eat after training</li><li>B) A client following a vegetarian diet</li><li>C) Food rules causing distress, secrecy around eating, or exercising to compensate for eating</li><li>D) A client who skips breakfast</li></ul><ol start=\"5\"><li>A client mentions a huge work deadline, four hours of sleep, and wants to test their max pull-ups today. You should:</li></ol><ul class=\"options\"><li>A) Run the test — motivation should be used when present</li><li>B) Cancel the session entirely</li><li>C) Run the session at reduced intensity with a technique focus, and reschedule the test</li><li>D) Test, but only a single set</li></ul>",
"1.13.1": "<p>Almost never from a single dramatic moment. Almost always from accumulation.</p><p>The four causes, in order of how often they appear:</p><ol><li>Volume spikes.</strong> The most common by a wide margin. A trainee who has been doing 60 pushups a week discovers motivation and does 300. The tissue was adapted to 60. This is also why a client returning after a break must restart lower than where they stopped, and why they will always argue about it.</li><li>Pattern imbalance.</strong> Too much pushing relative to pulling. Shoulders drift forward over months, overhead space narrows, pain appears. Slow, silent, entirely preventable — and the reason Module 1.1&#x27;s rule matters.</li><li>Skipped prerequisites.</strong> Dips without support holds. Pull-ups without scapular control. Handstands without wrist conditioning. Levers without a general strength base. The body can often <em>do</em> the movement before it can <em>tolerate</em> the movement.</li><li>Ignoring early signals.</strong> A small ache, trained through for six weeks, becomes a real injury. Almost every serious overuse injury had a two-week window where it could have been handled with a small adjustment.</li></ol>",
"1.13.2": "<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Injury</strong></th><th><strong>Where it hurts</strong></th><th><strong>Usual real cause</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Shoulder impingement</td><td>Front/top of shoulder, worst overhead</td><td>Push/pull imbalance + no scapular control</td></tr><tr><td>Medial elbow pain</td><td>Inside of elbow</td><td>Volume spike in pulling; forearm imbalance</td></tr><tr><td>Lateral elbow pain</td><td>Outside of elbow</td><td>Straight-arm work added too fast; weak extensors</td></tr><tr><td>Wrist pain</td><td>Front or back of wrist</td><td>Floor/handstand volume without wrist prep</td></tr><tr><td>Lower back pain</td><td>Lumbar spine</td><td>Poor bracing; hollow position never learned; hinge never trained</td></tr><tr><td>Knee pain</td><td>Front of knee</td><td>Volume spike in squats/jumping; poor knee tracking</td></tr><tr><td>Shoulder instability</td><td>Vague, &quot;slipping&quot; feeling</td><td>Extreme range without strength; often needs referral</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Read down the &quot;usual real cause&quot; column. <strong>Every single one is a programming decision.</strong> That is the encouraging part: injuries in this sport are largely a coaching problem, which means they are largely a solvable problem.</p>",
"1.13.3": "<ol><li><strong>Warm up properly. Every session.</strong> Twelve minutes. No exceptions, no &quot;I&#x27;m short on time today.&quot;</li><li><strong>Match every push with a pull.</strong> Or bias toward pulling for desk-bound clients.</li><li><strong>Earn the prerequisite before the movement.</strong> Support hold before dip. Active hang before pull-up. Wrist prep before handstand.</li><li><strong>Increase volume by no more than about 10% a week.</strong> Tissue adapts to load history, not to enthusiasm.</li><li><strong>Progress leverage slower than you want to.</strong> If you could move up two steps, move up one.</li><li><strong>Treat straight-arm work as its own category</strong> and build it very slowly.</li><li><strong>Condition the wrists and forearms deliberately</strong>, because nothing else will.</li><li><strong>Stop the set when form breaks</strong>, not when the target number is reached.</li><li><strong>Sharp or pinching joint pain means stop now.</strong> Muscular burn is fine. Joint pain is not.</li><li><strong>An ache that persists beyond two weeks is a referral</strong>, not a stretch.</li></ol><aside class=\"callout callout-myth-vs-reality\"><p class=\"callout-label\">MYTH VS REALITY</p><p><strong>&quot;No pain, no gain.&quot;</strong> Reality: no <em>discomfort</em>, no gain. Muscular burn, breathlessness, the shake of a hard hold — all fine and necessary. Sharp pain, joint pain, pinching, numbness, tingling — all signals to stop. Conflating these two categories is how people get hurt, and the phrase itself is responsible for a genuinely large amount of avoidable damage.</p></aside>",
"1.13.4": "<p>The professional boundary. Learn it now, because Stage 3 examines you on it and clients will test it constantly.</p><h4><strong>What you are qualified to do</strong></h4><ul><li>Screen movement and select appropriate exercises</li><li>Teach technique and correct form</li><li>Design and progress training programs</li><li>Coach effort, consistency, and motivation</li><li>Provide general education on recovery, sleep, and nutrition</li><li>Refer appropriately, and train around an issue someone else is managing</li></ul><h4><strong>What you are not qualified to do</strong></h4><ul><li><strong>Diagnose anything.</strong> Not impingement, not tendinitis, not a tear. You may describe what you observe; you may not name a condition.</li><li><strong>Treat injury or provide rehabilitation.</strong> Rehab is a physiotherapist&#x27;s work.</li><li><strong>Prescribe individual diets, calorie targets, or macronutrient splits.</strong></li><li><strong>Advise on medication or medical conditions.</strong></li><li><strong>Provide manual therapy, massage, or joint manipulation.</strong></li><li><strong>Give psychological counselling.</strong> You can listen, care, and refer. You cannot treat.</li><li><strong>Override medical advice.</strong> Ever. If a doctor said no overhead work, the answer is no overhead work — and if you disagree, the client can go back to the doctor.</li></ul><h4><strong>How to refer well</strong></h4><p>Referring is not an admission of inadequacy; it is the clearest possible signal of professionalism. But <em>how</em> you do it decides whether the client feels supported or dismissed.</p><p><strong>Do:</strong> &quot;I want to get someone who specialises in this to have a look — they&#x27;ll be able to tell us exactly what&#x27;s going on. In the meantime we&#x27;ll keep training everything that doesn&#x27;t bother it, so you don&#x27;t lose the progress you&#x27;ve built.&quot;</p><p><strong>Don&#x27;t:</strong> &quot;I can&#x27;t help with that.&quot; (Abandoning.) <strong>Don&#x27;t:</strong> &quot;Sounds like tendinitis.&quot; (Diagnosing.) <strong>Don&#x27;t:</strong> &quot;Just push through, it&#x27;ll settle.&quot; (Negligent.)</p><p>Build a referral network early: a physiotherapist, a sports doctor, a dietitian. Knowing three names you trust makes you dramatically more valuable to your clients, and it is the cheapest professional investment available to you.</p><aside class=\"callout callout-from-the-floor\"><p class=\"callout-label\">FROM THE FLOOR</p><p>A client says: &quot;My physio told me to avoid overhead pressing for six weeks, but I feel fine now and I want to do handstands.&quot;</p><p>Your answer is no, and it is not negotiable. You say: &quot;Your physio knows what&#x27;s going on in that shoulder better than I do. Six weeks isn&#x27;t long. Let&#x27;s use it — we&#x27;ll build your legs, your hinge, your core, and your horizontal pulling, and when you&#x27;re cleared you&#x27;ll come back to overhead work stronger than you left it.&quot;</p><p>This is the exact moment where a trainer either demonstrates professional integrity or gambles a client&#x27;s shoulder against their own desire to be liked. There is no version of overriding medical advice that ends well for you.</p></aside>",
"1.13-quiz": "<p>Five questions, ordered from straightforward to challenging.</p><ol><li>The most common cause of calisthenics injuries is:</li></ol><ul class=\"options\"><li>A) Pattern imbalance</li><li>B) Volume spikes</li><li>C) Skipped prerequisites</li><li>D) Poor footwear</li></ul><ol start=\"2\"><li>Weekly training volume should increase by no more than approximately:</li></ol><ul class=\"options\"><li>A) 5%</li><li>B) 10%</li><li>C) 25%</li><li>D) 50%</li></ul><ol start=\"3\"><li>Shoulder impingement in a calisthenics trainee is usually caused by:</li></ol><ul class=\"options\"><li>A) Excessive stretching</li><li>B) Push/pull imbalance combined with absent scapular control</li><li>C) Training legs too frequently</li><li>D) Insufficient protein intake</li></ul><ol start=\"4\"><li>Which of the following is OUTSIDE a trainer&#x27;s scope of practice?</li></ol><ul class=\"options\"><li>A) Screening movement and selecting exercises</li><li>B) Teaching technique and correcting form</li><li>C) Naming a client&#x27;s condition as tendinitis</li><li>D) Providing general education on sleep and recovery</li></ul><ol start=\"5\"><li>A client says their physiotherapist restricted overhead pressing for six weeks, but they feel fine and want to do handstands. The correct response is:</li></ol><ul class=\"options\"><li>A) Allow it, since the client knows their own body</li><li>B) Allow a reduced version as a compromise</li><li>C) Decline, keep training everything unaffected, and wait for clearance</li><li>D) Ask them to get a second opinion from another physiotherapist</li></ul>",
"1.14.1": "<p><em>Required for trainer-track certification. Worth reading even if you are training only for yourself — understanding how</em> <em>coaching works makes you better at coaching yourself.</em></p><p>You can know every progression ladder in this manual and be a poor coach. Knowledge is the entry requirement; transmission is the job.</p><p>Coaching is four skills: <strong>demonstrating, cueing, observing, and communicating.</strong> All four are trainable, and all four are more learnable than most people assume.</p>",
"1.14.2": "<p><strong>Show, then explain — not the reverse.</strong> A beginner cannot process a verbal description of a movement they have never seen. Show it first, cleanly, twice. Then explain.</p><ul><li>Demonstrate from the angle that makes the key point visible. Pushup form? Side view. Knee tracking? Front view.</li><li>Demonstrate at the client&#x27;s level, not yours. If they are doing incline pushups, demonstrate incline pushups. A flawless full pushup demonstration is not useful to someone who cannot do one — it just makes the gap feel bigger.</li><li>Show the fault deliberately too. &quot;Here&#x27;s what it looks like when the hips sag&quot; is often more instructive than another perfect rep.</li><li>Keep it short. Two demonstrations, then get them moving. Learning happens in their body, not in your demonstration.</li></ul>",
"1.14.3": "<p>A cue is not an explanation. <strong>A cue is the shortest possible input that changes what a body does.</strong></p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Weak cue</strong></th><th><strong>Strong cue</strong></th><th><strong>Why</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>&quot;Engage your core&quot;</td><td>&quot;Squeeze your glutes and make a straight line&quot;</td><td>Specific, physical, checkable</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Don&#x27;t let your elbows flare&quot;</td><td>&quot;Screw your hands into the floor&quot;</td><td>Gives an action instead of a prohibition</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Use your back, not your arms&quot;</td><td>&quot;Pull your shoulder blades down first&quot;</td><td>Describes a doable action, not an abstraction</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Go lower&quot;</td><td>&quot;Chest to my hand&quot;</td><td>Gives a target</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Keep your shoulders down&quot;</td><td>&quot;Make your neck long&quot;</td><td>Positive framing, easier to feel</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><strong>The rules of good cueing:</strong></p><ol><li><strong>One cue at a time.</strong> A beginner given three corrections fixes none. Pick the most important fault, fix it, then move to the next.</li><li><strong>Prefer external cues.</strong> &quot;Push the floor away&quot; (external) usually works better than &quot;contract your triceps&quot; (internal).</li><li><strong>Say what to do, not what to avoid.</strong> &quot;Knees out&quot; beats &quot;don&#x27;t let your knees cave.&quot;</li><li><strong>Give a target when possible.</strong> Something to touch, reach, or aim at.</li><li><strong>Cues are personal.</strong> A cue that transforms one client&#x27;s squat will mean nothing to another. Build a collection and experiment.</li></ol><aside class=\"callout callout-coach-s-eye\"><p class=\"callout-label\">COACH&#x27;S EYE</p><p>The most common new-trainer error is talking too much. You know a lot and you want to show it. But every extra sentence during a set is cognitive load the client spends on processing you instead of on moving.</p><p>Try this constraint: <strong>one cue per set.</strong> Watch the set, choose the single highest-value correction, say it in under seven words, and let them do the next set. You will be a noticeably better coach within a month.</p></aside>",
"1.14.4": "<p>You cannot correct what you cannot see. Train your eye deliberately.</p><p><strong>Where to stand:</strong> side-on for body line and range; front-on for knee tracking and symmetry; behind for scapular movement on pulls. Move between sets, not during them.</p><p><strong>What to look at, in order:</strong> 1. <strong>Body line first.</strong> Is the trunk holding position? 2. <strong>Range second.</strong> Is the movement complete at both ends? 3. <strong>Joint path third.</strong> Where do the elbows or knees travel? 4. <strong>Tempo fourth.</strong> Is it controlled or falling? 5. <strong>Breathing fifth.</strong> Held or flowing?</p><p><strong>Use timing as your diagnostic.</strong> From Module 1.3: a fault present from rep one is a capability or control problem. A fault appearing at the end of a set is fatigue, and the fix is a shorter set. This single distinction will make you look far more experienced than you are.</p><p><strong>Film things.</strong> The camera is the best coaching tool ever invented, for you and for the client. People cannot feel what their hips are doing. They can see it instantly.</p>",
"1.14.5": "<p><strong>Explain the why, briefly.</strong> A client who understands why they are doing inverted rows will do inverted rows. One who does not will quietly replace them with something more interesting. One sentence of reasoning buys enormous compliance.</p><p><strong>Manage expectations honestly and specifically.</strong> &quot;Most people get their first pull-up in two to four months of consistent work&quot; is far better than &quot;soon&quot; and far better than promising four weeks. Honest timelines build trust; optimistic ones destroy it on the exact date you named.</p><p><strong>Give feedback in a usable ratio.</strong> Tell them what worked, then the one thing to change. Not because criticism is harmful, but because a client who hears only faults stops being able to tell which fault matters.</p><p><strong>Match your language to the person.</strong> A physiotherapist client and a fifteen-year-old beginner need completely different vocabularies for the same instruction.</p><p><strong>Handle the ego conversation directly.</strong> Clients will want to skip steps, add weight, and film themselves doing things they are not ready for. Do not simply forbid it — explain the cost, give a timeline, and give them something to be excited about now. &quot;You&#x27;ll be doing that in four months. Right now, this is what gets you there faster than anything else.&quot;</p><aside class=\"callout callout-from-the-floor\"><p class=\"callout-label\">FROM THE FLOOR</p><p>A client repeatedly does half-range pull-ups so they can report a bigger number, and gets defensive when you count them differently.</p><p>Do not fight over the number. Reframe what the number means: &quot;Let&#x27;s track full-range reps — it&#x27;s a harder number, but it&#x27;s the one that tells us you&#x27;re actually getting stronger. You&#x27;re at four right now. Four full reps is a genuinely better place to be than nine half ones, and in six weeks your four will be eight.&quot; You have moved the goalpost to something honest while giving them a metric they can still be proud of.</p></aside>",
"1.14.6": "<p>Two things, both non-negotiable for a certified trainer:</p><p><strong>One: you must be able to do what you teach.</strong> Not at elite level. But a trainer who cannot perform a clean pull-up cannot coach one — they cannot demonstrate it, cannot feel what the client is feeling, and cannot credibly stand in front of them. This is why Stage 1&#x27;s practical standards are a gate and not a suggestion.</p><p><strong>Two: you must keep training.</strong> A trainer who stops training loses their eye, their credibility, and the direct experience that makes their coaching real. Your own practice is part of your professional competence, not a hobby alongside it.</p>",
"1.14-practical": "<ol><li>Write down five cues you currently use and rewrite each into a stronger form using the rules in 1.14.3.</li><li>Film yourself demonstrating the pushup, the inverted row, and the squat — each at both a beginner-appropriate regression and the full version.</li><li>Coach a real person through a full session from a program you wrote. Film it.</li><li>Watching that footage, list every fault you missed in the moment. This list is the single most useful thing you will produce in Stage 1.</li><li>Write out the explanation you would give a client for why they are doing inverted rows instead of more pull-ups — in under 40 words.</li><li>Practise the one-cue-per-set constraint for one full session and write a short reflection on what changed.</li></ol>",
"appendix-a": "<p>A complete, ready-to-run block. Three full-body sessions per week, on non-consecutive days.</p><p><strong>Every session begins with the 12-minute Stage 1 warm-up (Module 1.5) and ends with the 7-minute cool-</strong> <strong>down. These are not optional and they are not listed again below.</strong></p><p>Choose your ladder step for each pattern from Module 1.6 based on your screen results and baseline tests. The program structure below stays the same; the step you run it at is individual.</p><h4>Weeks 1–2: Learn the Patterns</h4><p>Volume deliberately low. The goal is technique, not fatigue. If you finish these sessions feeling you could have done much more — correct. You should.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Exercise</strong></th><th><strong>Sets × Reps</strong></th><th><strong>Tempo</strong></th><th><strong>Rest</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Vertical pull (your step)</td><td>3 × 5</td><td>3-0-1-0</td><td>2 min</td></tr><tr><td>Horizontal push (your step)</td><td>2 × 8</td><td>3-1-1-0</td><td>90 sec</td></tr><tr><td>Horizontal pull (your step)</td><td>3 × 8</td><td>2-1-1-1</td><td>90 sec</td></tr><tr><td>Vertical push (your step)</td><td>2 × 8</td><td>3-0-1-0</td><td>90 sec</td></tr><tr><td>Squat (your step)</td><td>2 × 12</td><td>3-0-1-0</td><td>90 sec</td></tr><tr><td>Hip bridge</td><td>2 × 12</td><td>2-1-1-1</td><td>60 sec</td></tr><tr><td>Plank</td><td>2 × 30 sec</td><td>—</td><td>60 sec</td></tr><tr><td>Side plank</td><td>2 × 20 sec each</td><td>—</td><td>45 sec</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Weeks 3–5: Build</h4><p>Double progression begins. Add one rep to one set per week, per exercise.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Exercise</strong></th><th><strong>Sets × Reps</strong></th><th><strong>Tempo</strong></th><th><strong>Rest</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Vertical pull</td><td>4 × 6</td><td>2-0-1-0</td><td>2 min</td></tr><tr><td>Horizontal push</td><td>3 × 8→10</td><td>2-1-1-0</td><td>90 sec</td></tr><tr><td>Horizontal pull</td><td>3 × 8→10</td><td>2-1-1-1</td><td>90 sec</td></tr><tr><td>Vertical push</td><td>3 × 8→10</td><td>2-0-1-0</td><td>90 sec</td></tr><tr><td>Squat</td><td>3 × 12→15</td><td>2-0-1-0</td><td>90 sec</td></tr><tr><td>Single-leg hip bridge</td><td>3 × 10 each</td><td>2-1-1-1</td><td>60 sec</td></tr><tr><td>Hollow hold</td><td>3 × 20 sec</td><td>—</td><td>60 sec</td></tr><tr><td>Side plank</td><td>3 × 25 sec each</td><td>—</td><td>45 sec</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Week 6: Deload</h4><p>Same exercises, same ladder steps, <strong>two sets each, and stop three reps short of where you would normally stop.</strong> Keep the warm-up and cool-down in full. This week is training, not a holiday.</p><h4>Weeks 7–10: Build</h4><p>Continue double progression. Expect one to two ladder-step advances across the block. When you advance a step, restart that exercise at 3 × 8.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Exercise</strong></th><th><strong>Sets × Reps</strong></th><th><strong>Tempo</strong></th><th><strong>Rest</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Vertical pull</td><td>4 × 6→8</td><td>2-0-1-0</td><td>2 min</td></tr><tr><td>Horizontal push</td><td>4 × 10→12</td><td>2-1-1-0</td><td>90 sec</td></tr><tr><td>Horizontal pull</td><td>4 × 10→12</td><td>2-1-1-1</td><td>90 sec</td></tr><tr><td>Vertical push</td><td>3 × 10→12</td><td>2-0-1-0</td><td>90 sec</td></tr><tr><td>Squat or split squat</td><td>3 × 15 / 10 each</td><td>2-0-1-0</td><td>90 sec</td></tr><tr><td>Hinge (your step)</td><td>3 × 12</td><td>3-1-1-1</td><td>60 sec</td></tr><tr><td>Hollow hold</td><td>3 × 30 sec</td><td>—</td><td>60 sec</td></tr><tr><td>Hanging knee raise</td><td>3 × 10</td><td>2-1-1-1</td><td>60 sec</td></tr><tr><td>Side plank</td><td>3 × 30 sec each</td><td>—</td><td>45 sec</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Week 11: Peak</h4><p>Highest volume of the block. Add one set to your vertical pull and horizontal push. Otherwise as weeks 7–10, at the top of your rep ranges.</p><h4>Week 12: Deload and Retest</h4><p><strong>Sessions 1 and 2:</strong> deload — two sets per exercise, well short of failure.</p><p><strong>Session 3: retest.</strong> Full warm-up, then re-run every baseline test from Module 1.4.3. Record the numbers next to your week-one numbers.</p><p>Then write your next block, starting from your new ladder steps.</p>",
"appendix-b": "<h4>Session log</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Date</strong></th><th></th><th><strong>Sleep last night</strong></th><th></th><th><strong>Energy (1–5)</strong></th><th></th><th><strong>Any pain</strong></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Exercise</strong></th><th><strong>Step</strong></th><th><strong>Set 1</strong></th><th><strong>Set 2</strong></th><th><strong>Set 3</strong></th><th><strong>Set 4</strong></th><th><strong>RIR</strong></th><th><strong>Notes</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Vertical pull</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Horizontal push</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Horizontal pull</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Vertical push</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Squat</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Hinge</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Core</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Anti-rotation</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><strong>Session notes:</strong></p><h4>Progression tracker</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Pattern</strong></th><th><strong>Week 1 step</strong></th><th><strong>Current step</strong></th><th><strong>Date advanced</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Vertical pull</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Horizontal push</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Horizontal pull</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Vertical push</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Squat</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Hinge</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Core</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Baseline test record</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Test</strong></th><th><strong>Week 1</strong></th><th><strong>Week 12</strong></th><th><strong>Week 24</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Max pushups</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Max inverted rows</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Max pull-ups</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Max squats</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Plank hold</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Dead hang</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Active hang</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Scapular pull-ups</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div>",
"appendix-c": "<p><strong>Active hang</strong> — Hanging with shoulder blades depressed and engaged.</p><p><strong>AMRAP</strong> — As Many Reps/Rounds As Possible within a set time.</p><p><strong>Anti-movement</strong> — Core work that resists motion rather than producing it.</p><p><strong>Archer variation</strong> — An asymmetrical movement where one arm works while the other stays extended; a bridge toward one-arm work.</p><p><strong>Bracing</strong> — Creating abdominal tension to stiffen the trunk.</p><p><strong>Closed chain</strong> — Movement where the working limb is fixed against an immovable surface.</p><p><strong>Concentric</strong> — The shortening/lifting phase of a movement.</p><p><strong>Deload</strong> — A planned week of reduced volume to allow recovery.</p><p><strong>Depression (scapular)</strong> — Pulling the shoulder blades down away from the ears.</p><p><strong>Double progression</strong> — Progressing reps to a ceiling, then advancing to a harder exercise and restarting at the lower rep count.</p><p><strong>Eccentric</strong> — The lengthening/lowering phase; where most strength adaptation occurs.</p><p><strong>Elevation (scapular)</strong> — Shoulder blades rising toward the ears; generally a fault under load.</p><p><strong>False grip</strong> — Gripping with the wrist over the top of the bar; used for muscle-ups.</p><p><strong>Hinge</strong> — Hip-dominant movement pattern with minimal knee bend.</p><p><strong>Hollow body</strong> — The foundational calisthenics position: lower back flat, ribs down, pelvis tucked.</p><p><strong>Impingement</strong> — Compression of tissue in the shoulder joint; typically pain overhead.</p><p><strong>Isometric</strong> — A contraction with no movement; a hold.</p><p><strong>Kipping</strong> — Using a hip swing to generate momentum in a pull-up.</p><p><strong>Leverage</strong> — The mechanical advantage or disadvantage created by body position; the primary variable in calisthenics.</p><p><strong>Negative</strong> — Performing only the eccentric phase, usually slowly.</p><p><strong>Open chain</strong> — Movement where the limb moves freely through space.</p><p><strong>Passive hang</strong> — Hanging with relaxed, elevated shoulders.</p><p><strong>Plane of motion</strong> — Sagittal (forward/back), frontal (side to side), transverse (rotational).</p><p><strong>Prime mover</strong> — The muscle primarily producing a movement.</p><p><strong>Progressive overload</strong> — Systematically increasing demand over time; the fundamental law of training.</p><p><strong>Protraction (scapular)</strong> — Shoulder blades sliding forward around the ribcage.</p><p><strong>Regression</strong> — An easier variation of a movement.</p><p><strong>Retraction (scapular)</strong> — Shoulder blades drawing together toward the spine.</p><p><strong>RIR (Reps in Reserve)</strong> — How many more reps could have been completed with good form.</p><p><strong>Scapulohumeral rhythm</strong> — The coordinated movement of shoulder blade and arm.</p><p><strong>Serratus anterior</strong> — The muscle that rotates the shoulder blade around the ribcage; critical for safe overhead work.</p><p><strong>Stabiliser</strong> — A muscle holding a joint or segment still while others produce movement.</p><p><strong>Static</strong> — An isometric hold; also one of the four competitive lanes.</p><p><strong>Street lifting</strong> — Bodyweight movements loaded with external weight; one of the four lanes.</p><p><strong>Supercompensation</strong> — The rebuilding of capacity beyond its previous level after recovery.</p><p><strong>Tempo notation</strong> — Four numbers describing eccentric, bottom pause, concentric, top pause.</p><p><strong>Valsalva manoeuvre</strong> — Holding a breath against a closed throat to maximise trunk stiffness.</p>",
"stage-1-certification-checkpoint": "<p>Complete every item below to progress to Stage 2.</p><h4>Written</h4><ul><li>[ ] Check Your Understanding — Modules 1.1, 1.2, 1.3, 1.5, 1.7, 1.8, 1.9, 1.10, 1.12, 1.13</li><li>[ ] Practical assignment — Module 1.4 (screening)</li><li>[ ] Practical assignment — Module 1.11 (three programs plus a 12-week block)</li><li>[ ] All seven progression ladders written from memory</li></ul><h4>Practical standards</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>Standard</strong></th><th><strong>Requirement</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Pushups</td><td>10, full range</td></tr><tr><td>Inverted rows</td><td>10, feet at hip height or above</td></tr><tr><td>Dips</td><td>8 full, or 30-sec support hold + 8 assisted</td></tr><tr><td>Pull-ups</td><td>3 full, dead hang to chin over bar, or 5 × 5-sec negatives</td></tr><tr><td>Squats</td><td>20, full depth, heels down</td></tr><tr><td>Single-leg hip bridges</td><td>15 each side</td></tr><tr><td>Plank</td><td>45 seconds</td></tr><tr><td>Hollow hold</td><td>30 seconds</td></tr><tr><td>Side plank</td><td>30 seconds each side</td></tr><tr><td>Scapular pull-ups</td><td>8, no elbow bend</td></tr><tr><td>Support hold</td><td>30 seconds, shoulders depressed</td></tr><tr><td>Passive hang</td><td>60 seconds</td></tr><tr><td>Active hang</td><td>30 seconds</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>All standards judged on <strong>form, not effort.</strong> A rep that does not meet the range and control requirements does not count.</p><h4>Trainer track — additional</h4><ul><li>[ ] Run a full seven-screen assessment on a real person and document it</li><li>[ ] Coach a real person through a complete session from a program you wrote</li><li>[ ] Submit video identifying and correcting at least two form faults, with reasoning spoken aloud</li><li>[ ] Demonstrate all five Module 1.7 scapular drills and explain what each trains</li><li>[ ] Demonstrate the full wrist protocol and explain each drill</li><li>[ ] Written scope-of-practice examination</li><li>[ ] Written referral scenario — the three-week shoulder pain case</li></ul><h4>A closing word</h4><aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p><strong>Do not progress on the written work alone.</strong></p><p>A trainer who has read about the pull-up and cannot perform one has nothing to teach. The practical standards are the gate, and they exist to protect the people who will one day put their bodies in your hands.</p><p>If you are not there yet: you are not behind. You are in the middle of the exact process this manual describes. Go back to your ladder, find your step, run the twelve weeks, and retest. The standards are not a judgement of you. They are a description of the floor — and everyone who now stands well above it was once below it.</p></aside><p><em>Stage 2 — Development: the four training lanes trained separately, intermediate skills (muscle-up, L-sit, back lever,</em> <em>handstand), programming for a specific goal, mobility and recovery in depth, and the first real layer of coaching another</em> <em>human being.</em></p>"
};
